DELETE FROM unit_detail WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet';
DELETE FROM chapter WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p>This book is a series of talks, delivered aloud to an audience and then set down in prose. It is not a treatise composed at a desk, but a spoken teaching, transcribed from the sessions as they were given. The one who speaks in these pages is their author: he addressed listeners directly, and his voice, rather than a scholar''s pen, is what carries the argument from first page to last.</p>
<p>Across five sessions the book makes a single journey. It begins by proposing that religion, at its root, is founded upon love rather than fear or mere obligation. From there it asks why messengers are needed at all, and what work they do that could be done no other way. It then turns to Islam not as a set of propositions to be argued, but as something to be lived and experienced. The fourth session dwells on the character of the Prophet himself, and the fifth draws the whole together, holding up his life as a model for a successful human life.</p>
<p>The reader who knows nothing of this subject will find here an accessible entry, one that moves from a first principle about the nature of faith toward a concrete example to follow. The tone throughout is that of a speaker among listeners: unhurried, direct, and concerned less with the machinery of scholarship than with what a person might take to heart and carry into his own days.</p>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'introduction to the book';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block">So, let''s begin. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَامِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُلْكِ التَّوْبَةِ إِلَى النَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ ذِي الشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لَهُ بِأَسَاطِيرِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ الْقَوَاعِدَ مِنْ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَتْبَاعِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِي بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ إِمَامِ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَاقِينَ وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ نَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. So, as Muslims, learning about our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, and learning about the respect and the deference that is due to him is extremely important, because the Prophet has rights over us. And so I want to begin by exploring a verse from the Quran and a hadith that complements the verse. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says in the Quran, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ إِن كُنتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ ٱللَّهَ فَٱتَّبِعُونِى يُحْبِبْكُمُ ٱللَّهُ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is commanding the Prophet to say, that tell these people, say, if you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, then follow me, meaning the Prophet, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will love you. Similarly, in a hadith, our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَا يُؤْمِنُ أَحَدُكُمْ حَتَّى أَكُونَ أَحَبَّ إِلَيْهِ مِنْ وَالِدِهِ وَوَلَدِهِ وَالنَّاسِ أَجْمَعِينَ</span>. None of you truly believe until I am more beloved to him than his wealth, his parents, his children, and even his own soul.</p>
<p>Now the question is, how is my belief in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, dependent on whether I love the Prophet or not? What does it even mean to love the Prophet? Because this is a very profound statement. The Prophet said that a man''s faith, his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, is not complete until we actually love him more than we love anything in the world, anything that''s precious to us. And he named those things, right? Parents, children, all people, including the self, because we all love ourselves. Jesus said, love your neighbor as yourself. And the assumption there in that statement is that we love ourselves. So love them like we love ourselves. And that''s part of human nature, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, he made us love ourselves. It''s a means for our survival. But what''s really interesting to me is that according to this verse and this narration, the key to a successful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is love.</p>
<p>Because that''s what he said. No one of you is a believer until you love me. So he connected love with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>. Now, what is love? Love is actually an amazing word in Arabic. There are many words for love in the Arabic language, but the essential word is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبٌّ</span>. In Arabic, that''s the essential word. Our Prophet is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللهِ</span>. He is the beloved of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. All messengers have titles, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَلِيمُ ٱللَّهِ</span>, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span>, because he spoke with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيلُ اللهِ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْرَاهِيم</span>, because he was the friend of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوحُ اللهِ</span> because he brought the dead to life. So all of these prophets, they received a title, but our Prophet is given the title <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللهِ</span>, the beloved of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And this title is obviously superior to all the other titles, because the one you love transcends speech. Because the nature of love is that it''s inexpressible, right? The English word love is an old English word that goes back to a Saxon word. And it relates to being pleased with something. Now, interesting enough, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is also related to love. How? Because you believe in what you''re pleased with, right? You believe what you love. The things that your heart accepts are the things that you believe.</p>
<p>So in Arabic, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبٌّ</span>, which is the word our Prophet used, no one is a true believer until I am beloved to him. If you look at the root meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبٌّ</span>, it actually means seed. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبًّا</span> is a seed in Arabic. Now, what do you have to do to make a seed grow? You have to nurture it. So love is a seed that''s nurtured with the knowledge of the beloved. The more you nurture the seed with water, the more it grows. The more you learn about the beloved, the more you will love the beloved. So as we get to know the Prophet and as we get to know him intimately, the deeper we will fall in love. And the deeper we fall in love with him, the more it will transform us. Because love by its nature engenders within us a desire to become what the beloved loves, right?</p>
<p>And then another beautiful aspect of a seed, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبًّا</span>, is that it produces an endless harvest. You begin by nurturing the seeds, which ends in a harvest, right? You gather the yield of one season, but then what happens the next season? It grows again. You harvest it, it grows again. Again, you harvest it, again it grows. So love or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> is the same, where it just keeps on growing indefinitely, there''s no end to it. It keeps increasing in intensity with time. One of the descriptions of love they say is it is friendship on fire. That''s how they describe love. That it is something that begins to consume you, right? It turns into .</p>
<p>And people have accomplished this at remarkable levels in our tradition. You know, we have people from our tradition who literally died in the love of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. That''s how much they loved him. They literally died. We have stories of people that bled tears of blood. And tears of blood, which is an old blues motif for people who know anything about American music. You know, there''s that song, I''m crying tears of blood for you. Tears of blood are actually real, because when somebody gets into this very intense state, those little blood vessels in the eyes, they burst. And so tears of blood is a real thing. I think in medical terms, I think it''s called hemolacria or something.</p>
<p>But there is something, I mean, poets use this all the time in their poetry, this concept of tears of blood. That just by remembering the places the beloved used to go, caused people to weep these tears of blood. So there are people in our tradition who have literally died from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. Now this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is another amazing word in the Arabic language. It comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشَاقَكَ</span>, which is a type of vine that begins to grow around a tree and strangles it, eventually killing the tree. So the metaphor is that, you know, this tree, it falls in love with this vine and it offers up all of its nourishment to feed its beloved, right? And starves to death in the process. So it''s such a beautiful metaphor. And this is something, you know, only someone who has ever experienced this type of true love can really appreciate these things. If you''ve never tasted love, I mean, this is going to seem ridiculous to you. But if you ask the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, right, if you ask the one in love, that starvation in the service of the beloved, to please the beloved, is like the most beautiful experience of his life.</p>
<p>The Arabs say, , that this type of crazy mad love is a deadly disease that can only be cured by a connection, by being connected with the beloved. It can only be cured by a union with the beloved, right? So the love of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> can really take hold of the heart as you get to know who he was and what he did for us. Now, when you love somebody, you constantly remember them, right? That''s one of the qualities of love. You constantly think about them. That''s what happens when you fall in love. You call them up, you know, oh, I''m just thinking about you. I can''t stop thinking about you, right? That''s real, isn''t it? It''s real.</p>
<p>I just can''t stop thinking about you. I mean, why is that pressing on the heart? Because you''re in love. And that''s what love is. You know, I just can''t stop thinking about you. You write poems. What is that? It''s a fusion that''s coming out of your heart to write these poems. Really? And people write the worst poems when they''re in love. But it doesn''t matter, because they would never do it if they weren''t in love. Because they would know how ridiculous it is.</p>
<p>But it doesn''t matter because, you know, there has to be some kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَآبِير</span> when you have this kind of love, you know, some kind of expression. in Arabic is what you express, right? The word , which is also used in Urdu, right? The word means a discerning lesson, like a moral lesson that you gain from an experience. And the word for a bridge in Arabic, it''s because it helps you cross over. So you get from one side of the mountain to another through that bridge. From one side of the river to another. So a discerning lesson is the same way. You cross over from the state of ignorance to the state of knowledge. And then that experience of that profound realization that you got out of that discernment, what does that do? It brings out the tears, right?</p>
<p>That''s why a tear in Arabic is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبَرَّ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبَرَّ</span> actually means a tear, because it crosses over from inside your eyes onto your cheeks. So you can see how all these different meanings, they connect with the same root. I mean, this is why I find Arabic such a beautiful language, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Also, you have that word, right? Even in Urdu, which means trust, comes from the same root, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبَرَّ</span>. Because trust is like a bridge between two people that keeps them connected. The path is open both ways. You break that trust, that bridge collapses, right? So this is Arabic linguistics, where everything is, you know, all the different variations that stem out of a root are always connected in meaning. Anyway, so what I was saying is that these emotions of love, right, they need a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَبِير</span>, they need an expression, they have to come out. They need to cross over from inside the heart and manifest into actions.</p>
<p>Because love has significance. And when something has significance, it begins to affect the heart. And this is why even the remnants of the beloved become valuable to the lover. Even the remnants. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيّ</span> poetry, this is ancient Arabic, you know, this is poetry of Imru al-Qais, where he says, let''s stop my friends, let''s stop and look here at where the beloved used to be. And let''s weep over the traces of the beloved, you know, just to look at these traces. I think these were people that really knew what love was. Seriously, they had a deep and profound understanding of love. They really understood love. And they were extremely intense in their love. Not like this modern day ridiculous love that is portrayed by Hollywood and Bollywood, in which this guy is chasing a girl around a tree and singing songs, or this kind of superficial love that begins in a cafe and ends in a bedroom. No.</p>
<p>That''s not love. That''s just lust. It has nothing to do with love. But these ancient Arabs, they really knew what true love was. That real experience of intense love. So, that''s one of the motifs of just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُكَاءٌ عَلَى الْأَطْلَالِ</span>, you know, just weeping over the places where the beloved used to be. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمُرُّ عَلَى الدِّيَارِ دِيَارِ لَيْلَى</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُقَبِّلُ ذَا الْجِدَارَ وَذَا الْجِدَارَا</span>. You know, I passed by the house of Laila, and I kissed the walls of the house. This is just so beautiful, right? I kissed the walls of the house, but it''s not the walls. It''s not the walls that have enraptured my heart, but the love of the one that used to live between these walls.</p>
<p>I mean, this is why we travel to Makkah and Medina, right? This is why we go for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِيَارَة</span>, to Najaf, to Karbala. It''s not the places. It''s not these stones. It''s the beloved who used to live there. It''s because the feet of the beloved walked that ground. And at the end of the day, this is what it''s all about, right? That''s all this is. I mean, these places, these walls, they''re just hiding the eternal love of God. That''s what this world is, according to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>ic theology. When you begin to recognize this underlying emotion of love, you realize that that is essentially what you love about this world, not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, right?</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> praised himself by calling him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَلَمِينَ</span> ‎, not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الدُّنْيَا</span>. Right? He said, praise and gratitude be to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> who is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبٌّ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمِين</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَمْ</span> is what the Arabs call <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الِاسْمُ الْأَعْلَى</span>, right? They call it a noun of instrument. This <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَمْ</span> is an instrument of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِلْمٌ</span>. It''s an instrument by which you gain knowledge, you come to know of something. And what you come to know using this instrument of this world is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْأَمِين</span>, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. This world, this universe is God''s vehicle for giving us knowledge. And this is why things are so important to us, right? This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشِيَّة</span>, possessions, are important. This is why property is one of the essential reasons why we were given the ﻿<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>. It is to protect property because possessions are important. Why? Because they give us meaning, right?</p>
<p>Your possessions are your instruments through which you create meanings in your life. And the Prophet, this is why the Prophet used to name all of his things. I mean, he didn''t have many things, but the things that he did have, he always named them because they had meaning. And that is what this world is to a believer. It''s all meaning. The whole thing is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَعْنَى</span>. The poet says, ... that this whole cosmos is just meaning set up in images. This entire universe is just meaning set up in images, and whoever understands that is from the people of discernment, the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَر</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says in the Quran, think deeply, reflect, look at this world with a discerning eye. That''s where you get <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبْرَة</span>. And then the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبْرَة</span> leads to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبْرَهَ</span>, you know, that''s when the tears come. Because tears, they always come from the perception of meaning. You know, somebody gives you a really meaningful gift. Why do you start crying?</p>
<p>Why does that happen? Because your heart gets moved by that. It''s not the gift. It''s the emotion with which that gift was given. That brings the tears out, right? So meaning is a great gift that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has given us. And this is why the Quran, it calls its verses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَات</span>, which actually means signs literally, you know, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> in Arabic is a sign. Now considering the fact that the Quran is a book, right, it''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِتَابٌ</span>, you would think that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> would have called this message, this text, sentences within paragraphs, within chapters, right? Because that''s what a book contains. But no, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> calls them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span>, which are signs. Why?</p>
<p>Because by definition, a sign or a symbol is something that points to something else. A sign is always a means to an end. It is never the end itself. If I see a sign that says hospital 10 miles ahead, right, my goal and my final destination is the hospital, not the sign that reads hospital 10 miles ahead. So the verses of the Quran are called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> because they are markers, they are signs pointing to something else. What are they pointing to? They''re pointing to the signs within the universe, signs within creations. So you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتُ الْقُرْآنِ</span>, the signs of the Quran, pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتُ الْكَوْنَيْنِ</span>, the signs within cosmos, within creation, within universe, within the soul itself. Now for a believer, what are the signs of the cosmos pointing to? They''re pointing him back to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتُ الْقُرْآنِ</span>, the signs of the Quran. So a believer is constantly in this loop. He''s moving between these two signs. The signs of the Quran direct him to the signs within the creation, and then the signs of creation point him back to the signs of the Quran, and together they both point to the creator of both, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>You see? That''s why it calls its verses <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَات</span>. Now, how do we interpret signs? The human being, unlike any other creation, has been given this gift of an intellect, which is essentially the tool to recognize signs. That''s what the intellect is, right? You see dark clouds? You recognize it as a sign that it''s going to rain. Right? You see smoke, you recognize it as a sign that there is going to be, there''s some fire or some heat somewhere. That''s what the intellect is designed to do. It''s the same thing with language. It is made up of signs and symbols, alphabets.</p>
<p>Right? Symbols put together. If I read a sentence, this is a book. I don''t just stop at seeing those individual letters and symbols. I put them together, right? The T, the H, the I, the S, right? Because on their own, they mean nothing. The letters mean nothing. The alphabets mean nothing. If I wasn''t illiterate or if I was unlettered, then all I would see is some symbols put together that mean nothing to me. But because I''m literate, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, I can interpret the letters.</p>
<p>I can put them together and I can read that what this is saying is this is a book, and I can understand what it means. So these symbols, they translate into meaning for me, right? Same way this universe and everything within it are signs that signify <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s oneness. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَاتٌ</span> of the cosmos and the self, they mean something. They read something. What do they read? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَتِنَا فِى ٱلأٓفَاقِ وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ يَتَبَيَّنَ لَهُمْ أَنَّهُ ٱلْحَقُّ</span>. We will keep showing them our signs in the horizons and within their souls until it becomes absolutely clear to them that all of this is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَقُّ</span>. All of this is a sign of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. That''s what the signs of the universe means. All of this is from God. And just as a literate person can read language, a literate intellectual, a believer, he can read this world. Believers can read their own souls.</p>
<p>So a believer is someone whose consciousness is literate. He can see what the signs of the world are pointing to. They''re pointing to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s existence, to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s oneness, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s reality, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s awesome majesty, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s immeasurable beauty, His infinite mercy, also His wrath. It points to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s kingship, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلْطَان</span>, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. So from that perspective, we all have access to the existence of God. I mean, you don''t even need the Quran to read this cosmos and come to the conclusion that it has a creator. If you are utilizing and fulfilling the power of your intellect. If you can read the signs. And once you reach that conclusion on your own that this cosmos has to have a creator, all the Quran does, it just clarifies things for you about the creator.</p>
<p>Who is he? What does he want? Why does he want it? That sort of stuff. Now, according to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>ic theology, what is the purpose of human beings? Why did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> create human beings? The ultimate purpose of creating human beings is to know God. To recognize the oneness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And to know God is to love God. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوْحِيد</span> then becomes the ultimate purpose of creation according to the Quran. That''s what God wants. He wants us to make him unique in his life.</p>
<p>‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ أَحَدٌ</span> ‎, say that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is unique. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> wants. What about us? What do we as human beings ultimately want? Not Muslims, just as human beings, irrespective of race, creed, religion, you know, what do we ultimately want? We all ultimately, what we''re searching for, what we''re looking for is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span>, felicity. We want to be happy. But in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>ic tradition, happiness is not a state. Happiness is not a state you can be in. Like you can''t be happy. You can feel happy.</p>
<p>But you can''t be happy at any point in your life. Happiness is actually the sum total of your life. It''s the end result of your life, whether you lived a happy life or whether you lived a miserable life. I mean, that''s the sum total. And that is calculated based on what you did in your life or how you lived your life, right? According to the Greeks, it was all about virtuous living, what they called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَتِهْ</span>, you know, which was a life of excellence. This word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَتِهْ</span> in our tradition, it translates to the same thing as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. You know, you have <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. So the whole purpose of life is happiness. There''s no other purpose. Our purpose is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span>.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span>, or this happiness, this felicity, unlike anything else in life is an end. Not a means. Everything else is a means. If you ask people what they want, you know, average people that haven''t really thought deeply about things, if you just say, you know, what do you want out of life? Well, you know, I want to have a family. I want to be successful. I want this degree. I want to make lots of money. Or, you know, I want to look sexy or have six pack abs or want to be fashionable, dress a certain way, be looked a certain way, whatever. I mean, they''ll give you a lot of different reasons. But then if you start probing those reasons, why do you want money?</p>
<p>Why do you want power? Well, it enables me to be free. You know, I can do things that I can''t do without money. Why do you want to be free? Why do you want to do those things? And if you keep probing that way, right, the end result is always, always happiness. Because in the end we want to be happy. We want to be in a state of contentment. So felicity, happiness, is the essence of what we as human beings are out for. But most people don''t realize that, and they don''t understand the difference between, you know, what''s really good and what''s apparently good. You know, like people think that fame is good in itself.</p>
<p>So there are all these people who just want to be famous. They want to be known. Or they think that stature is a thing that''s good to have. Or, you know, they think money will make them happy. So they''ll sacrifice the real good in their life. Like family, like health, like peace of mind, relationships, like character, like modesty, like courage, like generosity. These are the real goods, but they''ll sacrifice them for what they deem to be the ultimate good in life, what is apparently good.</p>
<p>Right now, what Revelation does is that it turns this idea on its head, that there is no <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. You can''t get it, because no matter what you have, you can lose it. That if you''re in good health right now, you can drop dead. If you''re rich, you can become poor. If you''re beautiful today, you''ll wrinkle and shrivel up in time. If you''re strong, you''ll become weak with age, right? History is filled with examples.</p>
<p>Even the things you have and you do enjoy right now, the means, you only enjoy them for a short amount of time before you just want something new. Something else, something more, right? I have the iPhone 11, can''t wait for the new iPhone 12. Why? Cause I think that''s what''s gonna make me happy. So we''re completely enslaved to these means, the wealth, power, fame, body, stature, how many likes we get on Facebook. And we don''t even realize how enslaved we are. Put your phone away for one hour, I challenge you. Turn your phone off for one hour, kids, right? My daughter starts having convulsions when her phone, the internet is not working. I mean, that''s addiction, that''s slavery. So these illusory means to the ultimate end goal of happiness.</p>
<p>In reality, these are chains, these are shackles that keep us trapped in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. So the Quran puts forth a proposition. It invites us to freedom from everything. You see? It calls us towards freedom from everything. How? By becoming the willing slaves of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It creates this paradigm shift. It switches our focus from these millions of means that we think are going to take us to the end of happiness. It switches our focus on one central entity, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the ultimate end goal of happiness. It doesn''t stop us from acquiring things, but it changes the perspective and the meaning of those things, that I can enjoy everything this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الَمْ</span> has to offer, because it brings me closer to the knowledge of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">العَلِيم</span>.</p>
<p>And so once we submit to him, once we channel all our distracted energies and focus it on this one central entity, we become free. By submitting to the one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, we become free of all other submissions, all other slaveries, resulting in a life and an afterlife that is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span>, with never-ending felicity. This is why the Quran says we did not create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span> or man except for willing servitude. We did not create <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span> or man except for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَعَادَة</span>, except for happiness, which can only be achieved through a willing submission to the Creator.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> is a very powerful and comprehensive word in Arabic. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> refers to everything that is ontologically dependent on God, whether they believe in him or not. An <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> is that which cannot exist on its own, which is everything, right? So everything besides <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> from that vantage point, because of its dependence on him. But then the other meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span> is those that embrace that dependence, those that accept that dependence by directing themselves to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, by worshiping and serving <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So there are these two levels of servitude in the meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>. One is ontological dependence, which all creation share. And then a willing dependence by those selected for devotion, which only the people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> share.</p>
<p>Now as human beings, we can only willingly submit to the one we love. Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>, the second meaning, the more profound meaning of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبْد</span>, means willing submission. And we can only willingly submit to the one we love. That''s how willing submission works. It cannot be forced. You cannot force anyone to love.</p>
<p>You cannot put a gun to someone''s head and say be a Muslim. It doesn''t work that way. You have to submit. This is why you always fall in love. It''s a falling. I have never heard anyone say I rose in love. They always say I fell in love, right? Submission, it absolutely requires love. But in the process of falling, in that process of submission, you rise in the eyes of the beloved. Isn''t that true? In the process of falling, in the process of that submission, you elevate yourself in the eyes of the beloved. How?</p>
<p>By becoming the best version of yourself to make the beloved happy. Isn''t that true? You become obsessed with pleasing the beloved. And in that process, you free yourself from pleasing everybody else. Everyone else, you don''t care. All you care is the pleasure of the beloved. You don''t care about anything else. ... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَيْتَ الَّذِي بَيْنِي وَبَيْنَكَ عَامِرٌ، وَبَيْنِي وَبَيْنَ الْعَالَمِينَ خَرَابُ، إِذَا صَحَّ مِنْكَ الْوُدُّ، فَالْكُلُّ هَيِّنٌ، وَكُلُّ الَّذِي فَوْقَ التُّرَابِ تُرَابُ</span>. If I get the sweetness of your love, I don''t care if my entire life becomes bitter. Right? If you are pleased with me, I don''t care if everybody becomes angry with me.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَيْتَ الَّذِي بَيْنِي وَبَيْنَكَ عَامِرٌ وَبَيْنِي وَبَيْنَ الْعَالَمِينَ خَرَابُ</span>, as long as what''s between you and I, as long as our love is flourishing, this world can become derelict, you know, I don''t care. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا صَحَّ مِنْكَ الْوُدُّ فَالْكُلُّ هَيِّنٌ</span>, if I have your love, then everything is beautiful to me. Otherwise, everything on the face of this earth is just dust to me. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. I mean, that''s what love does, right? That''s the power of love.</p>
<p>I saw this advertisement for learning Italian a few years ago. It was one of those language learning DVDs, and you know, they showed this farm boy who''s in love with this Italian celebrity, and they tell him that he''s gonna have one chance to meet him. I don''t know if you''ve ever seen this ad, but they tell him that you''re gonna have one shot at meeting this celebrity, so he wants to learn Italian to be able to speak to her, just for one moment. This was just an ad, right, of learning language, but whoever designed it, he really understood love. Because when you''re in love with someone, you will do anything. You will go to any extremes for the beloved.</p>
<p>You''ll learn a whole new language just to spend one moment with the beloved. I really hope you experience true love at some point in your life, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> made love in this world. He gave us this gift of love. This worldly love, right? He made it to show us what divine love is supposed to be like. He gave us love to make us understand how to love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. It''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span>, a proof against us. You know these stories of Laila Majnun or Romeo and Juliet. These are proofs against us. Because if this is the love of a created thing for another created thing, then what should be the love of a creation for his Creator? Right? If that''s what, you know, if that''s what you''ll do, if you''ll go to these lengths for another person, then how far should you go for your Creator?</p>
<p>And this is where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> comes into the picture for us. You know, this is where his submission, his servitude for his creation comes into play. He loved <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> so much that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> made His love a necessary condition for the acceptance of our love. Say if you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> then follow me and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will love you. Why? Because loving our Prophet will transform us into someone that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will love. Which, by the way, is a very inverted concept if you think about it, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> should be the beloved because He''s the Creator, and our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he''s just a creation. Creation is supposed to love the Creator, not the other way around. But what we learn from the Quran is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is in love with this man <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. What does that tell you about this man? Quran is filled with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s expression of His love for His Prophet. These names, these beautiful titles that He uses to address His messenger. He swears by things. I mean, that is love.</p>
<p>Why does <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> love this man so much? Because he used his free will to submit to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, and in the process he transformed himself into the best of creation. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> fell in love with the qualities exhibited by this man. This is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is. This is what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is all about. This is what ﻿<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span> is all about. We have a religion that is based in love, that is full of love. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is a love-based religion, and anyone who tells you differently has not understood this religion. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, I swear to you, the one thing I have consistently found in my years and years of study is love.</p>
<p>We as creation are an expression of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s love. Everything the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> did for us was out of pure love. If you read his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>, that is the one consistent emotion you''ll find behind every action. And he didn''t need to do this. He didn''t need to do any of it. You know, just as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, as the creator, did not need to fall in love with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, who was his creation, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span> did not need to love us. He did not need to suffer and face hardships in his life to guide us. He did not, you know, he didn''t need to lead us out of darkness into light, but he did it anyway. Why? Because of that love he had for us, that selfless love. And so it is only fair to return love with love, because you cannot love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> without getting to know him and without falling in love with his beloved, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>This is why he said none of you are true believers until you love me, until you get to know me, because I am the one that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> fell in love with, because loving me will naturally transform you into someone that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> loves. Love is a fire, right? And fire softens and it bends steel. Fire allows the ironsmith to mold a shapeless, useless piece of steel into something beautiful, which then graces the mantle of your house, right? Burning in the fire of our messenger''s love is going to mold us into something beautiful that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> would want in his presence.</p>
<p>This is why he is the greatest sign, the greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, a living role model, the one that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> wants us to emulate. And as I said, love will naturally engender submission, and through our willing submission to the free, by submitting to the one who is free, to the high, we ourselves will become free. And we will elevate ourselves to realize our greatest potential for which we were created, which is true happiness, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَدًى</span>, felicity, the ultimate purpose of life.</p>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'love based religion';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ وَمُمَلِّكِ التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ ذِي الشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ وَكَمَالِ النَّبِيِّينَ وَخَاتَمِ سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ عِيسَى ابْنُ مَرْيَمَ وَدَعَا لَهُ بِأَسَاسِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ فَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ وَعَلَى أَتْبَاعِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ وَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ الْغَالِبِ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ مَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ وَنَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللهِ وَرَسُولُهُ أَرْسَلَهُ اللهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللهِ شَهِيدًا وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيَّ بْنَ أَبِي طَالِبٍ وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ فَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلِّمْ عَلَيْهِمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ فَمَا بَعْدُ أَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ قَوْلِهِ أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. In today''s session, we''re going to look at the purpose and the need for sending messengers, which are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُول</span>, right? So when I say purpose, I mean purpose from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>''s perspective and need from our perspective.</p>
<p>So the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُول</span>, it comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسَلَ</span>, which means to transmit or to correspond or to delegate forward. So the idea is that this message or this book, it comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>, but then they''re delivered to us through an intermediary or these intermediaries that are called messengers or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُرْسَلِينَ</span>, the plural form. So then after believing in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>, His book, His angels, the next thing we have to believe in as Muslims is the messengers.</p>
<p>Now when these messengers, they deliver <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>''s message to humanity, there is a response to that message. It is either met with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> or it is met with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span>. It is met with acceptance or rejection.</p>
<p>Now this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span>, you''ll almost always see translated in books as disbelief. So a ***** is used for a non-believer or a disbeliever. And that actually is not the correct translation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span> and it''s not a good translation. If you look at the root meaning of this word, right? It comes from, which means to conceal, to hide. The active participle of this root is, which actually means a farmer. A in Arabic literally means a farmer. Why? Because what a farmer does is that he covers a seed with dirt. He conceals the seed with dirt. He puts the seed into darkness.</p>
<p>So the idea there is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span> is placing a covering over something. That is the essence of it. So in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>ic sense, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span> is actually seen as truth concealed. And the idea there is that in reality, everyone recognizes the truth. No one can be a disbeliever. You can either accept the truth, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, or you can conceal it, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفْر</span>.</p>
<p>And this is why the greatest truth concealer in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>ic worldview is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span>. He was actually one of the closest to God, and he knew the truth. He recognized that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آدَم</span> was superior to him, but then he concealed it, using his rationalizations of fire being better than clay.</p>
<p>Now one of the problems with modern Muslims is that they assume that everybody is a ***** if they''re not a Muslim, right? These verses in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span></p>
<p>that are talking about the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفَّار</span> are not talking to everybody who''s not a Muslim. They''re talking to people who are arrogant, who are puffed up, who are filled with pride, and who wouldn''t give you the time of the day. I mean, read the descriptions of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُفَّار</span> in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. They''re <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُتَكَبِّر</span>, they''re <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُخْتَالٌ فَخُورٌ</span>, they''re <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظَالِمٌ</span>, right? I mean, these are the attributes that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> gives them in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Most people that he talks to in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> are simply a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصٌّ</span>, right? A lot of them have simply just not heard the message, or they''ve not properly understood it, or they''re just in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, you know, they''re in the state of heedlessness.</p>
<p>And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> refers to these people as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُنْس</span>. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> first calls people <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا النَّاسُ</span> before he calls them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ</span> or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا</span>. He simply calls them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا النَّاسُ</span>. If you look at the chronology of the revelation of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> did not initially call the Arabs <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ</span>, because they weren''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَافِرٌ</span>. But then after seeing miracles after miracles and witnessing everything that they had witnessed, and after treating the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> the way they treated him for doing nothing other than calling them to their own success, that''s when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> said, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ الْقَوْلِ</span>. No, this is too much. You''re not going to get away with this. And that''s when the verses started turning into <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُلْ يَا أَيُّهَا الْكَافِرُونَ</span>. So that''s a very important distinction to make.</p>
<p>Now, what''s interesting is that there are many ways to conceal the truth. And certainly many, many Muslims are truth concealers in reality. So I can be a Muslim, and here I am doing all the right things, fulfilling my obligations, all of that. But in reality, I''m concealing the truth. How is that? The essence of truth concealing is ingratitude. The essence of truth concealing is ingratitude, not being grateful. And this is why a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَافِرٌ</span> is also called an ingrate in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> says, you know, He gave you everything that you asked for. If you try to enumerate the blessings of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>, you will not be able to count them. Indeed, a man is extremely unjust and he is an ingrate.</p>
<p>The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَّرَ</span> here in this verse does not mean disbeliever. It means an ingrate. The idea is that the real reason for sending messengers is good intention. In other words, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> has a good intention towards his creation, and this is why he sent messengers. If God simply created the human being and just threw away the manual and said, work it all out for yourselves, we would all spend our life in turmoil and darkness, and we would have no clue what to do or where to go. Like that Mullah Naseeruddin. I mean, I don''t know if this new generation, if you''ve ever heard that name or not, but I know people of my age, and we grew up reading these Mullah Naseeruddin stories. It''s a Middle East character that they tell stories using him. So in one of these stories, Mullah Naseeruddin was in a tavern all night, and he goes out early morning, like wee hours of the morning. He goes out and he''s wandering the city just aimlessly, right? And this policeman comes up to him and says, Mullah, what are you doing out here at this time?</p>
<p>And he says, if I knew the answer to that question, I''d be home in bed. Right? But this is the state of many, many people. They''re just literally wandering around in a drunken stupor, and they have no clue what they''re doing. And so all they do is they just try to keep themselves just constantly distracted, right? Constantly entertained. Rumi talks about the many wines that God has put on this earth to put people out of their misery, you know, these distractions. He says, Jesus was drunk in the love of God, and his donkey was high on barley. So there are many, many ways that people are trying to deal with this human condition, which is dealing with the pain of existence. And then they develop this mindset like, you know, John Lennon said, whatever gets you through the night, right? So there are many people that have this attitude. I''m just</p>
<p>gonna get through this life, whatever it takes. That, from a Muslim perspective, is a type of ingratitude because the messengers have come. They have given people news. They have given people guidance, but they don''t wanna accept it. It''s like if you''re thirsty and I give you directions to the river and you say, &quot;No, you know, I''m just gonna watch some Netflix or I''m gonna play a video game and I''m just gonna try to forget that I''m thirsty.&quot; That''s ingratitude. But then there is a whole bunch of people who are trying to distract themselves. Now, another Mullah story that kind of is another way of looking at this is that Mullah Naseeruddin was looking around one day for something. And somebody came by and he said, &quot;Mullah, what are you looking for?&quot; He says, &quot;Some keys that I lost.&quot; So this man starts helping him out, right?</p>
<p>And after about an hour or two, he says, &quot;Listen, where exactly do you think you lost them?&quot; So Mullah Naseeruddin, he says, &quot;Oh, way over there.&quot; So this man says, &quot;Then what are you looking here for?&quot; And he says, &quot;The light is better over here.&quot; Right? There''s this idea that people will go where it''s easy instead of going where it''s hard. And this is also considered a type of truth concealment, to go where it''s easy instead of going where it''s hard. And this is where we begin to dilute religion to make our own lives easy. And this is why these messengers, you know, they are first and foremost, they are human beings. And this is why they present this incredible example for us through their character, by following the book of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> to the letter, setting this precedent for us that it is humanly possible to achieve what the book says.</p>
<p>Another interesting aspect of prophethood is this idea that we have forgotten our true purpose. We''re all distracted by the shine and the glimmer of this world. And this kind of relates to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, which means heedlessness or forgetfulness.</p>
<p>That the human being by his nature is just forgetful. And if you look at this in terms of the analogy of the Vanity Fair, right? The servant is sent by the king to go to the marketplace and get something. But on the way to the marketplace there''s this Vanity Fair. You know, &quot;Oh, I''ve got plenty of time, sun''s high, I''m going to go look at the fair.&quot; So he goes into the fair and then suddenly, entertainment, right? Suspension of purpose. And he gets preoccupied and he just loses himself in the fun, and before he knows it, the sun''s gone down. &quot;Oh my God, the king sent me to the marketplace to get this thing, and the marketplace is closed, and I wasted my time in the Vanity Fair.&quot; So this type of metaphor is related to this human condition, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. That we become immersed in a type of heedlessness because this world by its nature is powerful. It''s powerful in the sense that we get preoccupied. So how do we remind ourselves? How do we pull ourselves out of the Vanity Fair and remind ourselves daily that this world is temporal and there''s a journey coming? From the Muslim perspective, it is the reminder itself, a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>. This is why the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is called a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span>, because it keeps reminding us of our true purpose in life.</p>
<p>Now, contrary to our forgetful natures, messengers are awakened beings. They''re awakened beings. All prophets, they share four common qualities. The first is truthfulness. They''re always truthful. They never lie. The second is trustworthiness. They''re trustworthy. If he says he''s going to be there tomorrow, he''s there. The third is conveyance, that they have this ability to articulate and convey the message. And the fourth is wakefulness, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَتْنَة</span>. Messengers are wakeful beings.</p>
<p>So these messengers, they are individuals who have woken up to the truth. They are like the cave dwellers of Plato''s analogy. If you''ve read the Republic, in the seventh book where Plato talks about justice, truth, and things like beauty, he gives this allegory of a group of prisoners who have been confined in a cavern since their birth. And they have no knowledge of the outside world. They are chained facing a wall and they''re not able to turn their heads around. And there''s a fire burning behind them, giving off a light and casting shadows on the wall in front of them. Now, occasionally people pass by, you know, this fire behind them, carrying these figures or animals and some other objects, and these objects, they cast a shadow on the wall, which the prisoner sees as reality. So they begin naming and classifying these illusions. And then suddenly one prisoner is freed and he walks out of the cave for the first time. And the sunlight begins to hurt his eyes and he finds this new environment completely disorienting. And then when he''s told that the things around him are actually real and the shadows in the cave, they were just reflections.</p>
<p>He cannot believe it because these shadows, they appeared so much more clear to them. But gradually his eyes adjust until he can actually look at the reflections in the water. He can literally see the objects directly. And finally he can see the sun, which is the ultimate source of light for everything. So this freed prisoner, he returns to the cave to share his incredible discovery with the others. But since he''s no longer used to the darkness, he now has a hard time seeing the shadows on the wall. And these other prisoners, they think that this journey has made him crazy and he''s blind and he has no idea what he''s talking about. And so they violently resist all his attempts to free them. Very interesting passage. And Plato introduces this passage as an analogy of what it is like to be a messenger. What it is like trying to educate people.</p>
<p>The challenges they face when they come to wake people up out of their slumber and their distractions. They face these enormous resistance, right? So the cave in his passage is this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. And the world outside the cave is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. Everything that we see in this world, every encounter, every emotion, every event, it''s a reflection of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. But we believe it as being real. It''s just casting a shadow of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آخِرَة</span>. But most people are so comfortable in their ignorance that they actually become hostile to anyone who points it out to them. But then what''s worse is that these people are not even interested in examining the proposition. And that''s what''s called acedia. The word acedia is a Latin term that''s generally translated as sloth.</p>
<p>It''s actually a Christian theology term. The seven cardinal sins, one of them is sloth. So they translate acedia as sloth, which means laziness or some resistance to work. But in the Christian theology, acedia did not originally mean laziness. You know, people that are lazy in their work. Acedia, it actually meant spiritual laziness. That people weren''t interested in finding out the truth. And that''s just arrogance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> calls these people <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقْمَحُونَ</span>, right? In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ يس</span>. And the term <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُقْمَه</span> is a term taken from camels. The Arabs say that, you know, when they would take their camel to the water, why would they take their camel to the water? To help it out, right?</p>
<p>Quench its thirst. But what the camel would do is it would raise its head up and refuse to go down and drink. That''s a . The messenger takes them to the river of knowledge to quench their thirst, but they''re too arrogant to bow down and take the drink. And that is arrogance. And I''ve met so many of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, why should I read that book? Well, because one out of every four people actually think it''s from God. That seems like a pretty good reason just to be interested in it, right? Just to read it and see maybe there is something there. Give it a chance. But no, you know, too arrogant to even examine the proposition.</p>
<p>So human beings by default are in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, which is the opposite of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذِكْر</span> or remembrance. And this is why in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> these two terms are always juxtaposed, right? Heedlessness, remembrance. Heedlessness, remembrance. They are focused on the outward appearances of things, but they are completely heedless about their afterlife.</p>
<p>Now, when the prophets come, they tell the people, they give the people a formula. And that formula is . There is no god except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. There is no entity, nothing worthy of submission or worship or love except <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. And the belief in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is that no messenger has ever come except that he has said <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. Indeed the religion with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> has always been <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>, submission. So every messenger that has come from the beginning of time has come with this exact same message, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. That is a universal message for all prophets. What will change is the second part of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَهَادَة</span>. For the Muslims it is now <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُّحَمَّدٌۭ رَّسُولُ ٱللَّهِ</span>. At one time it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِيسَى رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>At another time it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>. Jesus is the messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. Moses is the messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. Noah is the messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. So this idea from the Muslim perspective is that all of humanity has been given these messengers to remind them about God and get them out of their state of heedlessness, from, you know, cure their state of acedia.</p>
<p>Now why are they doing that? To understand that, let''s examine this. Our religion, right, our Prophet''s religion is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الْإِسْلَامِ</span>. So let''s break this term down. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span>, right, it comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامًا</span>, which means to submit, to willingly submit to the love of God. And we talked about this yesterday, that you cannot submit until you love, right? Love is a precondition for submission. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> means to submit, to willingly submit to the love of God. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> in Arabic, it comes from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَنٍ</span>. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ضَنٍ</span> in Arabic means a debt. And so our religion is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الْإِسْلَامِ</span>, which literally translates to the debt of willing submission.</p>
<p>The debt of willing submission. In other words, the idea is that we have been endowed with innumerable blessings by our Lord. And for those blessings, we owe Him a debt of gratitude through our willing submission. That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الْإِسْلَامِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَفَغَيْرَ دِينِ ٱللَّهِ يَبْغُونَ</span>. Do they seek anything other than the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِين</span> of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُۥٓ أَسْلَمَ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَوَتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ طَوْعًۭا وَكَرْهًۭا</span>. When all creatures in the heavens and the earth, they have bowed down to Him in submission. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَوْعًا وَكَرْهًا</span>. Willingly or unwillingly. So what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> is asking rhetorically is that, do these people seek other than repaying their debt? When in reality, everything in existence is paying back its debt to its Creator by being in a state of submission, willingly or unwillingly.</p>
<p>We see that the sun is in a state of submission. It will rise and it will set according to what has been decreed for it, right? It''s consistent. You can calculate sunrises and sunsets for the next 10 years. Now, what is the power of a human being in comparison to the sun? We''re insignificant, right? And yet this immense celestial ball of fire, many times the size of this earth, is in a state of submission to its Creator. It''s fulfilling its purpose. But the human being is not. And same goes for everything else in the universe, right? Galaxies, solar systems, sun, moon, stars, oceans, rivers, mountains. They''re all in a state of submission.</p>
<p>They''re doing what they were created to do except the human being. Now, there are areas of willing or unwilling submission that even we as human beings, we recognize, right? One of them is the call to nature, right? We have to do certain things that we cannot avoid. One of them is to eat. That is part of our nature. When we get hungry, we must eat or we die. We need to drink water when we''re thirsty. If we don''t drink, we die. We have to relieve ourselves once we have eaten or taken drink. We don''t have a choice in these matters. We have to sleep.</p>
<p>When sleep comes upon us, there''s only so much we can fight it. Eventually we have to submit to it or we''ll go into the state of acute psychosis. So there is this idea of submission to nature by itself. And then there is also submission to our own beings, to our physical bodies, our limitations. We can only eat so much. We can only drink so much. We can only do so much physically before we''re exhausted, right? So we have limitations in our movements because just the way our bodies are designed, the structure of our creation. So there is a type of submission of the self, given that we are temporal creatures and we''re limited by the physical aspects of our nature. So in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, there is this idea that everything is in a state of submission, including us. Everything in the heavens and the earth are in a state of submission, willingly or unwillingly. But then it is followed by a reminder.</p>
<p>The same verse, it continues: &quot;And to Him is their return.&quot; Now, if you look into creation, things are emerging and then they are returning into non-existence. Everything that has life that comes into existence, it returns back to non-existence. Plants, vegetables, animal kingdom, ultimately even the mineral kingdom. All of this is returning back to non-existence after it had come to existence. So what we''ve established so far is that everything in existence is paying back their debt of submission to their Creator, whether it''s willing or unwilling, and they are returning back to the Creator. But the only species that is not paying back this debt is the human being. And then there''s a difference. The difference is that the human being is asked for willing submission. The human being, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنْسَان</span>, the believer, is asked for willing submission, which is out of love, not by force. Not — same word is used over there: there is no compulsion in religion, there is no force in religion. So that''s one difference compared to every other species, that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> is asking us for our willing submission.</p>
<p>The others, the rest, they are in submission whether they like it or not, whether they want to or not. We have been given free will. Another difference is that human beings are not asked about their debts right now. Nobody is holding a gun to my head for repaying this debt that I owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>. When will we be asked about this? On the Day of Judgment, right? Who will ask us about it? Who''s going to take us to account for this debt? In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْفَاتِحَةِ</span>, right, the fourth verse, , the owner and the king of the day.</p>
<p>That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِك</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِك</span> is the one who''s an owner and a king. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَلِك</span>, the owner and the king, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الدِّينِ</span>, of the day when the debt of willing submission falls due. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الدِّينِ</span> is the day when the debt of submission falls due. It''s payback time. How you transact with others is the way you will be transacted with. It''s also called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْحِسَابِ</span>, the day of accountability. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> says in one of the hadith, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِبُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ</span>...</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَاسِبُوا قَبْلَ أَنْ تُحَاسَبُوا</span>. Take yourself to account before you are taken to account. Now, here''s something really interesting. There''s a debt of submission that we owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> for everything that He''s done for us. But then there is also a debt that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> owes us.</p>
<p>Isn''t that interesting? So just as we owe <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> a debt, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> owes us a debt as well. See, when the master issues a command to his slave, right, the fulfillment of that command is a debt on that slave. Now there are only two possibilities. He either obeys the command or he disobeys the command. If I ask my daughter to bring me a glass of water, she has only two choices. She will either bring me the glass of water or she will not bring me the glass of water. In both cases, right, a return falls due on the master. If the slave is obeyed and he fulfills his obligation, his master now owes him a reward. And if he disobeyed the command, then he owes him a punishment. And that''s essentially <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الدِّينِ</span>. That''s the day when the debt falls due on both, on us as well as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. And this is why He says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, who will indebt <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> with a beautiful debt? Why is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> asking us for this debt, for this loan? So that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> multiplies its reward many, many times over and returns it back to His slaves. So in this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is asking us, His slaves, that who among you will give Me a loan? A loan of good deeds, a loan of submission, a loan of obedience.</p>
<p>Who will indebt Me so that I can multiply its reward many, many times over and pay you back? For He has the most generous rewards, the most noble rewards. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. So to summarize, this wake-up call is essentially the purpose of sending messengers. To wake us up, to wake humanity up. Because we''re in a state of heedlessness. Our day of reckoning is drawing near. We''re losing sand in our hourglass every second, and we have absolutely no worries about that day. We are drowned in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. Let''s examine this word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, actually, because it''s quite an interesting word, and, you know, this might get too technical, but it''s very interesting. I find these things very interesting. Because in Arabic linguistics, right, whenever a word begins with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْن</span> — <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> begins with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْن</span> —</p>
<p>It usually implies some sort of a covering, you know, or something being hidden or something being veiled like , right? They all have this meaning of being hidden or veiled or confined, like , right? It''s confined. So the idea is that our intellects have been veiled by the desires and temptations of this world, this . We''re unable to use our intellects. You know, a simpleton or a fool in Arabic is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغَفَّل</span> because they''re very easy to trick. You know, like people that fall for these Ponzi schemes, generally they would be called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغَفَّلٌ</span>. So that''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> means. Somebody who, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُغَفَّل</span> is somebody whose intellect has been veiled. The other meaning of the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is the inability to digest milk, which is also very interesting. It is the inability to digest milk, lactose intolerance.</p>
<p>Because milk is one of the most beneficial things in this world for mankind, right? And so to not accept that benefit, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. And this is nothing against the lactose intolerant, right? It''s just imagery to create concepts, because Arabic words have images rather than meanings. But that''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is. And our scholars have said that all of sin, all wrongdoings are rooted in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>. That''s the basis of it, that if you were really aware of God, you could never sin. You know, if God was present in your heart, it would be impossible for you to sin. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, oh my human being, what has put you in the state of delusion about your generous Lord? You know this , I need a whole session to explain. I swear, I''m not even kidding. I''m not exaggerating this.</p>
<p>, the love in the statement, I need a whole session to explain it. From a linguistic perspective. Oh my beloved human being, what has put you in this state of delusion about your generous Lord? A scholar said, he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> put the answer to that question in the question itself. What has taken you away from your generous Lord? What makes you think you can do all these things and that you''re not going to be taken into account for it? What did it? Where''s the answer? Where''s the answer in the question? Your generosity. Your generosity is what has made me delusional.</p>
<p>I am heedless about sinning because you don''t react immediately with punishment. You''re extremely generous with me. I mean, that''s why I sin. The people you love the most and the people who are the most generous with you, you take them the most for granted, isn''t it? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> is so generous in his gifts to creation that we just forget that he''s our master. And that''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span> is. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَرَمٌ</span>, his generosity, his immense mercy has made us bold.</p>
<p>You know, there''s this amazing hadith, I just suddenly remembered, in which our Prophet , he describes this conversation between <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> and his angels regarding human beings. It''s such a — you know, this was one of, I remember, one of the most life-changing things for me. But according to this narration, the oceans, the earth, and the angels, they are complaining to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>, right? And it goes something like, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا مِنْ يَوْمٍ إِلَّا وَالْبَحْرُ يَسْتَأْذِنُ رَبَّهُ أَنْ يُغْرِقَ ابْنَ آدَمَ، وَأَنَّ الْأَرْضَ تَسْتَأْذِنُ رَبَّهَا لِتَبْتَلِعَهُمْ، وَالْمَلَائِكَةُ تَسْتَأْذِنُ أَنْ تُعَاجِلَهُ وَتُهْلِكَهُ</span>. That not a single day passes, not a single day goes by that the oceans do not ask permissions from its master. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, give me permission to drown them. They enjoy your countless blessings and then they dare disobey you. Just say the word.</p>
<p>Let me teach them a lesson. Let me show them your power. Let me show them your wrath. The earth begs its master. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, give me permission. I''ll swallow them all. They walk on your earth, eat from your blessings, live upon it and then not prostrate to you. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span>, give me permission to remove them from the face of this earth. The angels: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا اللَّهُ</span>, give us permission. It will take us an instant. We will annihilate this whole lot of arrogant people in a moment. Who do these people think they are, standing against you?</p>
<p>You know, scholars have explained that these oceans, the ocean, the earth, the angels, these entities, these are creations of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> who absolutely love their creator because they''re in complete submission. And they are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شُهَدَاء</span>, they are witnesses. They''re constantly witnessing their creator being disobeyed, being constantly rejected, being made fun of. How would you feel?</p>
<p>How would you feel if your loved ones, people that you love were constantly being humiliated and rejected? Wouldn''t that make you angry? Right? So in their anger, in their frustration based on what they''re seeing, they call upon <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> for his permission to destroy us. They are creation just like us, right? And they want destroy us because of our arrogance. But our Creator, the one who we are disobeying, the one who we are humiliating, the one who we are ignoring, He says to them, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالرَّبُّ تَعَالَى يَقُولُ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَعَا عَبْدِي، فَأَنَا أَعْلَمُ بِهِ إِذَا أَنْشَأْتُهُمْ مِنَ الْآيَاتِ</span> He says, He replies to them, you know, leave my slaves alone. I know everything about them since the moment I created them from this earth. If they are your creation if you created them he''s talking to the ocean he''s talking to the earth the angels if you created them do whatever you want with them drown them swallow them destroy them do whatever you want if they are your creation but if they are my creation then hand them over to me and walk away I swear by my power and my majesty if they come to me in the night I will accept them if they come to me in the day I will accept it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَإِنْ تَقَرَّبَ مِنِّي شِبْرًا تَقَرَّبْتُ مِنْهُ ذِرَاعًا، وَإِنْ تَقَرَّبَ مِنِّي ذِرَاعًا تَقَرَّبْتُ مِنْهُ بَاعًا، وَإِنْ مَشَى إِلَيَّ هَرْوَلْتُ إِلَيْهِ</span> If he takes a step towards me, I will move an arm''s length towards him If he moves an arm''s length towards me, I will move a yard towards him If he walks towards me, I will run towards him.</p>
<p>If he asks, if he seeks my forgiveness, I will forgive him and when he repents on his sin, I will accept his repentance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">من تقرّب إليّ لقيته من بعيد، ومن أعرض عني ناديته من قريب</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنْ تَقَرَّبَ إِلَيَّ لَقِيْتُهُ مِنْ بَعِيدٍ، وَمَنْ أَعْرَضَ عَنِّي نَادَيْتُهُ مِنْ قَرِيبٍ</span> When He approaches me, I rush from afar to welcome Him. And when He turns away from me, I walk up to Him and I say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَوَلَكَ كَرَبٌ كَوْنَ غَيْرِي؟</span> Do you have a Lord that is better than me? ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَن جَآءَ بِٱلْحَسَنَةِ فَلَهُۥ عَشْرُ أَمْثَالِهَا ۖ وَمَن جَآءَ بِٱلسَّيِّئَةِ فَلَا يُجْزَىٰٓ إِلَّا مِثْلَهَا وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُونَ</span> ‎ Don''t you see that when you come to me with one good deed, I reward you ten times over for that deed, and when you come to me with one sin, I respond with just what is due to remove it. And even then so many times I choose to forgive it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. This is our Lord. This is our loving merciful creator. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>.</p>
<p>Who told you that our Lord is cruel? Why do we have this impression in our minds that he''s just someone waiting to burn us in hell? He created us with his own hands according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. He honored us by having the angels prostrate to us. What more do we want? You think he enjoys burning us in fire? I mean, seriously. Those of you who have children, right? Your children make these ridiculous drawings, these stick figures when they''re little. And you proudly stick it up on your refrigerator. You show it to the world. You put it on display.</p>
<p>You didn''t even create your children. You just gave them birth. Mothers didn''t do any work in developing the fetus in their wombs. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> did it. And yet you would never do, never do anything to hurt your child. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللّٰه</span> enjoys hurting us. We are His greatest, His most beloved creation and you think He wants to cause us pain? Where do you get that information? I don''t understand. Who made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الإِسْلَام</span> so ugly for you? Who turned this incredibly loving Creator into a monster for you? I''m shocked when I talk to some of ...</p>
<p>... my beloved human being, what has put you in this delusional state about your generous Lord? You know, I really don''t know what more to say about this. It''s just ..................</p>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'need for messengers';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرْغَبُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَا يَبْلُغُ مِدْحَتَهُ الْقَائِلُونَ، وَلَا يُحْصِي نَعْمَاءَهُ الْعَادُّونَ، وَلَا يُؤَدِّي حَقَّهُ الْمُجْتَهِدُونَ، الَّذِي لَا يُدْرِكُهُ بُعْدُ الْهِمَمِ، وَلَا يَنَالُهُ غَوْصُ الْفِطَنِ، الَّذِي لَيْسَ لِصِفَاتِهِ حَدٌّ مَحْدُودٌ، وَلَا نَعْتٌ مَوْجُودٌ، وَلَا وَقْتٌ مَعْدُودٌ، وَلَا أَجَلٌ مَمْدُودٌ، فَطَرَ الْخَلَائِقَ بِقُدْرَتِهِ، وَنَشَرَ الرِّيَاحَ بِرَحْمَتِهِ، وَوَتَّدَ بِالصُّخُورِ مَيَدَانَ أَرْضِهِ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى سَيِّدِ الْأَنْبِيَاءِ وَالْمُرْسَلِينَ، خَاتَمِ النَّبِيِّينَ، السَّيِّدِ الْمُمَجَّدِ، الْبَشِيرِ الْمُصَدَّقِ، النَّذِيرِ الْمُؤَيَّدِ، الْمُصْطَفَى الْأَمْجَدِ، الْمَحْمُودِ الْأَحْمَدِ، أَبِي الْقَاسِمِ مُحَمَّدٍ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى وَصِيِّهِ عَلِيِّ بْنِ أَبِي طَالِبٍ أَشْرَفِ الْوَصِيِّينَ وَأَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا الْإِمَامِ الطَّيِّبِ، صَاحِبِ الدَّعْوَةِ النَّبَوِيَّةِ، وَالصَّوْلَةِ الْحَيْدَرِيَّةِ، وَالْعِصْمَةِ الْفَاطِمِيَّةِ، وَالشَّجَاعَةِ الْحُسَيْنِيَّةِ، وَالْعُلُومِ الْبَاقِرِيَّةِ، وَالْمَعَاقِلِ الْجَعْفَرِيَّةِ، وَالْحِكْمَةِ الْإِسْمَاعِيلِيَّةِ، وَالْجُودِ الْمُحَمَّدِيَّةِ، صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِمْ وَسَلَّمَهُمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرَا، اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْ لَنَا إِلَى مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ، اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا، اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ، اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ دَعْوَةٍ لَا يُسْتَجَابُ لَهَا وَمِنْ مُنْكَرَاتِ الْأَخْلَاقِ وَالْأَعْمَالِ وَالْأَهْوَاءِ، فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَقُولُ أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي، اللَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَعُوذُ بِكَ أَنْ أَضِلَّ أَوْ أُضَلَّ، أَوْ أَزِلَّ أَوْ أُزَلَّ، أَوْ أَظْلِمَ أَوْ أُظْلَمَ عَلَيَّ، وَأَجْهَلَ أَوْ يُجْهَلَ عَلَيَّ، اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللهُ، اللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ، آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> which is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثُ جِبْرِيلَ</span>. It''s a very famous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span>, where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> comes to the Prophet in the form of a man. And he asks him four questions. What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>? What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>? What is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>? And then he asks him about the signs of the end of time. So when, I mean, you can look it up and, you know, you can get all the information about it.</p>
<p>I''m just going to talk about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, that when he was asked about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, the Prophet replied, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَامُ أَنْ تَشْهَدَ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَأَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is to bear witness that there is no God but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. And Muhammad <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> says, Messenger. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَتُقِيمَ الصَّلَاةَ</span>, to establish prayer. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَتُقْتِيَ الزَّكَاةَ</span>, to give alms. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَتَصُومَ رَمَضَانَ</span>, to fast the 30 days in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَتُحَجَّ الْبَيْتُ إِنِ اسْتَطَعْتَ إِلَيْهِ سَبِيلًا</span>. And to perform the pilgrimage to the House of God if you have the means.</p>
<p>Now what''s interesting about this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> is that rather than giving a definition of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, which I would have expected, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, he described it as a set of practices. In other words, what he''s saying is that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is work, right? But what we forget is that this is not how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> started. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> as a human phenomenon, it began with an incredible experience for the Prophet and then finally culminated in a set of practices. For most of us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> begins as a practice. It never begins for us as an experience, but that''s not how it began for the Prophet.</p>
<p>How did it begin for him? It began with a direct experience of the noumenal realm, right? So you have the phenomenal realm, which is this world. This world of appearances or what we call the seen world, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمُ الشَّهَادَةِ</span>. And then there is the noumenal realm, which is the unseen world, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَمُ الْغَيْبِ</span>, which constitutes the metaphysical realities like God, angels, heaven, hell, all those things. And the Prophet experienced both in his lifetime. He had the phenomenal experience in the cave of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِرَاء</span>. And then he also had the noumenal experience with his ascension, right, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>.</p>
<p>So in today''s session, what I wanted to do is I want us to connect with both those experiences. Sort of step in his shoes and try to understand what it must have felt like. Because his experiences, particularly these two experiences, they set a precedent for us in terms of what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is meant to feel like. We all know what it is in terms of practice, but it''s important for us to understand what the feeling or the experience of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is as it was for the Prophet. So that once we connect with that, then we will talk about how we can use his experience for our own benefit.</p>
<p>So the year is 620 AD. Our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he''s 40 years old. Socially, the culture that he lives in is very troublesome for him. He''s bothered by the behavior of these <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيّ</span> Arabs, especially idol worship, because he had a natural disinclination towards superstitions or any kind of animism for that matter. So he''s troubled, right? And when you''re troubled, what do you do?</p>
<p>You retreat. So he wanted to get away from it all. And in order to do that, he made it a habit to go to this cave within a mountain called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَبَلُ النُّورِ</span>, which literally means the mountain of light. And the cave itself was called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حِرَاء</span>, which means darkness. And I find that interesting, right? A cave named darkness within a mountain named the mountain of light. Anyway, so he gets into this regular practice of contemplation and reflection, which today we call meditation, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَنُّث</span>, which is emptying the mind. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَنُّس</span> comes from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>, which in Arabic means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, attributing partners with God. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْوَاقِعَةِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, these people, they insist on committing great <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>. So, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>, attributing partners. In Arabic morphology, there''s something called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَابَ تَفَاؤُل</span>, where you add a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طه</span> before the word and the meaning morphs into avoidance of.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>, as it''s used in the verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَنُّث</span> means the avoidance of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّبْ</span> means side or nearness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَجَنُّب</span> means to avoid somebody''s nearness. So there are a lot of words like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَجُد</span>, which means sleep. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَهَجُّد</span> means to avoid sleep. Anyway, these are all these different words that follow the same pattern and morphology. So one day, this 40-year-old man, he''s busy meditating in this cave at the top of this mountain. And just like any other day, he gets into this deep contemplative state, and his desire is this attempt to empty out forms. That''s what he''s trying to do. He''s trying to disconnect himself from this physical world.</p>
<p>He wants to free himself from all of these forms or attachments of this world. Now, what''s the greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> of all? What is the greatest <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِرْك</span> of all? The greatest, it''s the worship of the self. Idolatry has nothing on self-olatry. I just made that word up. But really, idolatry has nothing on self-worship or egocentricity. According to our tradition, worship of the self, which pretty much everyone is engaged in today, it''s the most destructive thing there is for the soul. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> talks about these people who worship their desires and their passions. Do you not see the one who took his own desires, his own passions as his God? Do you not see the one that when his desire commands, he just simply submits? Because that''s worship.</p>
<p>So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> is in this cave in deep reflection, working on himself. He''s trying to empty his heart out of all these attachments. And even in Chinese, you have this thing, empty your cup so that it can be filled with something new. Because the soul has to be emptied of all forms before it can gain knowledge of the one who has no form. Because forms, they impinge on that understanding. And as he''s doing that, suddenly he sees the horizons filled with the presence of an angelic being. And this being says to him, Muhammad, I am <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> and you have been chosen as the messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So he raises his head to see who''s speaking to him, and he sees <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> filling up the horizons. Now think about that for a second. If all of us stepped out right now, and we saw an angelic presence from one horizon to the other and as far up in the sky as we could see, how would that affect us? How would that affect us psychologically?</p>
<p>How would that affect us physically? What would you and I experience in that moment? Would we be terrified? Would we be horrified? Would we be shocked? What would we experience? See, the interesting thing about experience is that it is not a logical proposition. You cannot prove or disprove experience. You can discuss it, you can talk about it, you can try to interpret it, but you cannot say whether it''s true or false. Because experience is always real for the one experiencing it. The challenge then is to determine whether or not that experience has an objective reality or it has a subjective reality. In other words, is that experience really a true experience, or is it something that is just happening to this individual as a type of psychosis?</p>
<p>Because we do have a lot of people in psychic wards today speaking to God directly, right? They will tell you that they speak to God and they can hear God speaking to them. These people are not lying, because even though they are in a delusional state, they actually believe what they''re experiencing. Their delusions are very real to them. So the Prophet naturally is filled with all those human emotions: fear, shock, horror. So he turns his face away from him, but no matter where he turns, he sees the angel in the exact same form. And then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> says to him, &quot;Recite.&quot; And he replies, &quot;What should I recite? I don''t know what to recite.&quot; And at that point, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> wraps his wings around him and he squeezes him three times.</p>
<p>And according to the Prophet, this squeeze was so hard that he felt stifled to the point where he thought he would die. And scholars have commented that this squeezing was a physical manifestation of a spiritual reality, which was removing any and all remnants of this world from his soul. He was being emptied out in order to later be filled with three divine vessels: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span>, and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْسَان</span>. And so there had to be a complete emptying out before the filling could take place. And this is what he was seeking. That''s what he was trying to do to begin with, with that meditation in . And also this is what the companions used to say about the Prophet, that he would literally empty us out of ignorance, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَاهِلِيَّة</span>, and then fill us up with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. Right? And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span> starts with a negation. There is no God. It starts with a negation for emptying out, breaking false pretenses, breaking false understandings.</p>
<p>Then it says <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِلَّا اللَّه</span>, which is an affirmation. So every <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> always began with, every religion began with an emptying out. And so even for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> was actually a complete emptying before it was a filling. And this in Arabic is called <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخْلِيَة</span>. The Arabs say, that the emptying out must precede the ornaments. In other words, you have to be stripped off of what is ugly before you can be ornamented with what is beautiful. So that squeeze was meant to empty his soul out. And then he says, then he starts filling him with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحْيٌ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَقَ ٱلْإِنسَنَ مِنْ عَلَقٍ</span> ‎. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ وَرَبُّكَ ٱلْأَكْرَمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِى عَلَّمَ بِٱلْقَلَمِ</span>. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَّمَ ٱلْإِنسَنَ مَا لَمْ يَعْلَمْ</span> ‎.</p>
<p>Recite. Recite in the name of your Lord who created you. Created man from a clinging substance. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱقْرَأْ وَرَبُّكَ ٱلْأَكْرَمُ</span>. Recite, for your Lord is the most generous. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ٱلَّذِى عَلَّمَ بِٱلْقَلَمِ</span>. Who taught by the means of the pen. Who taught man that which he did not know. So now the Prophet moves into a new realm, and a troublesome realm, I think. Because for every human being, for every intellectual human being at least, one of the fundamental crises of religion becomes, is my object of devotion indeed a transcendent reality? Or is it some kind of a distortion of my human consciousness?</p>
<p>In other words, is this experience really true? Or am I having some kind of a psychological breakdown? Now, how do you differentiate between a true divine experience and a demonic experience? And this is key, so pay attention to this, because this is the key that differentiates between a demonic experience and a true spiritual experience. A demonic experience is always accompanied with an expansion of the self. A demonic experience always begins with, you know, it''s a very exciting, very joyful experience, very pleasurable experience, but then it always inevitably results in pain and despair in the end. And you can think of anything that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> deems <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَرَم</span>, right? Drugs, alcohol, sexual deviancy, smoking, any kind of overindulgence. They all begin with a rush, an excitement, ecstasy, can''t stop feeling high, euphoria, but it ends, always ends</p>
<p>in suffering. I mean, and this is not just, you know, like major things like alcohol, that those end in like whatever, hangovers and DUIs and liver diseases. Drugs end in addictions, in ruining families. Smoking ends in cancer, heart diseases. But when the person actually starts smoking, when he starts drinking or whatever, right, it''s the coolest thing ever. It''s like awesome. That psychological high is intense, but it never ends well. And it doesn''t have to be this drastic, like food, gluttony, right? If there is something delicious in front of me and I pig out, right? If I stuff my face with it to the point where I can''t breathe, how do I end up feeling? While I''m eating it, while I''m tasting it, it''s like really pleasurable, I''m enjoying everything of it. But when I get that sick, full feeling, how does that feel, right?</p>
<p>So every kind of demonic experience, any kind of extreme is always gonna end up in pain. This is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> said that, the middle path is the best for everything. So the point is that every demonic experience begins with joy and a sense of elation, right? This expansion, but then it ends in constriction and misery. Now, on the contrary, a true spiritual experience is exactly the opposite. The initial experience is always incredible constriction, extreme fear, dreadful feeling, you know, the pressure of hard work, but it always ends in a sense of joy and expansion. And this is just a principle. It''s not a — like, it''s not limited to Muslims. Take any man who has ever achieved any greatness in this world, like Mahatma Gandhi, Mother Teresa, you know, Martin Luther King, famous painters, poets, anyone. Their initial experience is a struggle, suffering, hard work, but their final experience is just this incredible expansion, fame, glory. People celebrate them years after they die.</p>
<p>You know, they''re gone. Now, in this experience of our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> and the experience of all Prophets before him, we find the exact same reaction to revelation. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> tells us that the first divine words for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> were what? Don''t be afraid. Because his first experience was filled with fear when he saw God on the mountain. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> also had the same experience, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ مَرْيَم</span>, the mother of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>, terrified when she saw the angel. I seek refuge of the most compassionate from you. She got scared out of her mind. And so this is something we find consistent in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> and also what the Jews and the Christians believe in their traditions, that every religious experience is always overwhelming, it incapacitates the self. So when <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> received this first revelation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اِقْرَأْ</span>, it was a very painful process for him. And this is what I want you to connect with.</p>
<p>That this is not, you know, someone who just enjoyed the whole process of prophethood. He went through a lot of pain because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span> did not feed him the words that he repeated to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرَيْش</span> later on through this rote memorization. What was being delivered to him were the meanings. And these meanings were being directly breathed into him. The Arabic word for breath and for spirit, it''s the same, it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رُوح</span>. They come from the same root. So when these meanings, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحْيٌ</span> was being just blown into him, breathed into him, it was a very painful experience. Imagine, just imagine being given mouth to mouth by a giant. What do you imagine that would feel like? Probably like every cell of your body is being taken over by this immense pressure of that air, right? And then you have this feeling of complete helplessness while that''s happening.</p>
<p>So even as that breath is giving you life, it would feel like it''s squashing the life out of you, suffocating you under its enormous weight. That pressure, right? This is why, according to the narrations, in the early period of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, our Prophet would start convulsing when he received revelation. Because it was a very, very painful experience for him. And the disbelievers would use that as an excuse to say, look, he''s possessed by a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span>, look, he''s crazy. But in his own words, in the Prophet''s words, he describes the process. He says, it felt as though the message was being engraved on my heart. Engraved, that''s the word he used. Have you seen anyone ever do an engraving? Imagine that agonizing pain of a sharp blade carving deep inside your heart as you lie helplessly beneath it. You''re completely conscious of the pain, but you are unable to struggle against it.</p>
<p>Just imagine that, that is how difficult this process of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحْيٌ</span> was for him initially. But later on, it got a lot easier for him to the point where people would, they wouldn''t even know when revelation came upon him. So after the Prophet had this very first experience of revelation, this very first difficult experience of revelation, he went into a state of extreme shock. Lesley Hazleton, an incredible author, and if you''ve ever read her book, The First Muslim, she beautifully portrays his emotions after this first encounter with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span>. She writes, he did not come floating off the mountain as though he was walking on air. He did not run down shouting Hallelujah and Bless the Lord, I am your prophet. He did not radiate light and joy. There were no choir of angels, no music from the heavens, no elation, no ecstasy, no golden aura surrounding him. He did nothing that might seem as the reaction of a man who just crossed the border between this world and the spiritual world. That is such a beautiful description. See, if he had done that, right?</p>
<p>If he came down the mountain feeling top of the world, shouting hallelujah, that would have indicated signs of psychosis. Because this is how a crazy person speaking to God behaves in the psych ward. So, he had no signs or symptoms of a demonic experience. On the contrary, she writes, he was convinced that what he had encountered could not be real. See, such a human reaction. He was convinced that what he had encountered could not be real. He thought it was a hallucination or a trick of the senses. He thought he had been possessed by an evil <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِنٌّ</span>. So the man who fled down the mountain of Hera trembled, not with joy, but with a stark primordial fear. He was overwhelmed, not with conviction, he was overwhelmed with doubt. Now, if you think about this, right, this is exactly our initial encounter with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. A feeling of this constriction, that feeling of doubt.</p>
<p>Is this real? Is there really a God? Is there an afterlife? Is there heaven? Hell? And then it''s, you know, followed by the burden of practice. You know, when you take <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحَادِيثُ شَرِيفٌ</span>, right? Look at that, that pressure you feel. That''s the same experience as he had at Hera, right? It''s that burden of responsibility. Am I going to be able to do this? What if I''m not able to do this?</p>
<p>That''s expected with any spiritual experience. That''s the point I''m trying to make, that it always begins with a constriction. So scholars say that what happened up there on the mountain, just the sheer humanness of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>s reaction, that is the strongest argument for its historical reality. Whether you believe that the words he heard came from inside himself or from outside, it is clear that he experienced them. And he experienced them with the force that shattered his sense of being. You know, normally when people go into shock, they lose their ability to think. We know this, right? But with the Messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span>, it was the opposite. He was able to maintain his focus and presence of mind. How do we know that? The proof of that consciousness is in what he said to his wife, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَضْرَتْ خَدِيجَة</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامُ اللهِ عَلَيْهَا</span>, when he returned home.</p>
<p>What did he say? The first words that came out of his mouth when he returned home, what did he say? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَمِّلُونِي زَمِّلُونِي</span>, which means wrap me up, wrap me up. Now we know that people who experience shock, one of the first things the paramedics will do is they''ll wrap them up, right? You''re actually told to wrap them up to create some sort of containment, because part of the shock is a loss of the self, because the outward and the inward, they crash. That experience, that psychic integrity that we all have of the self being whole, it gets completely shattered when we''re in shock because something has forced itself on the soul. And the Prophet realized that, and so he asked his wife to wrap him up, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. So this was his first encounter with God, with revelation, and that feeling of intense constriction that he experienced. And his very first human response to that experience while maintaining his perfect consciousness. And scholars say that this is a proof that the experience was a divine spiritual experience and not a demonic one. Now let''s look at his second divine experience, which is the night journey of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْرَاء</span>, right?</p>
<p>And that actual ascension to the heavens, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَرَجْ</span>. The first experience was establishing a connection with the noumenal realm. The second was actually making a journey from the phenomenal realm to the noumenal realm. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ الَّذِي أَسْرَىٰ بِعَبْدِهِ لَيْلًا مِنَ الْمَسْجِدِ الْحَرَامِ إِلَى الْمَسْجِدِ الْأَقْصَى</span>. Glorified is the one who took his slave by the night from the sanctuary in Mecca to the sanctuary in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقْصَى</span> in Jerusalem. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَارَى</span> in Arabic means to travel in the night. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَثَرًا</span> is used when someone takes you by the night. You''re not traveling alone, but somebody is taking you. And one of the benefits of using the word night in its singular form, it''s because the Arabs, it took them 40 nights to make the journey from Mecca to Jerusalem. So the verse is emphasizing, highlighting that this journey took place in one single night. Now this migration, interestingly, has two aspects.</p>
<p>The first was a physical aspect in which a steed named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرَاق</span> was brought for the Prophet. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرَاق</span> is defined as a steed that was smaller than a horse and bigger than a mule. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البُرَاق</span> comes from the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْق</span>, which means lightning. It also means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span>, which means luminous. So this was a luminous steed that moved at the speed of light, because it went, according to traditions, according to the narrations, it went from one horizon to the other in one step. So it traveled at the speed of light, which we can understand that today. But in those days, that was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِمَامَةٌ</span>, right? So even though it was a physical journey from Makkah to Jerusalem, it took place almost instantaneously. It was at the fastest speed that is possible in the physical world, in this phenomenal realm. But then once he reaches the Mosque of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَقْصَى</span>, the actual ascension, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>, that''s a metaphysical journey. It was beyond the speed of light.</p>
<p>It was beyond time and space. You know, today in modern physics, we know that there are things like wormholes, where you can be in one part of the universe and then you go through this wormhole and you''re instantly transported to another part of the universe, right? Thanos in the Avengers. So, I mean, we can understand these things today, but these were unimaginable ideas back in the day, and they were completely unthinkable. So this was the experience of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>, it means an instrument of ascension, like a ladder or a stairway. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَجًا</span>, the root word, it means a ladder. So the first aspect of the journey, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِسْرَاء</span>, was a physical journey, even if it was at the speed of light. But it was a physical journey. The second one, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>, was beyond the speed of light, where he had a witnessing of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> says, he came so close to God, he came so close to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> until he was at a distance of two bows'' length with God, right?</p>
<p>So if you have a bow and arrow and you put two bows one in front of the other, that was the distance between him and his Lord. And scholars say that, I mean, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span> and all of that behind that, without going into any of that, the general description that scholars have given is that it''s an, it''s the two bows is an honorific expression of how close the Prophet SAW got to his Lord without breaching the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَب</span>. Because you know, in those traditions, in that time, the closest you could get to a king was the distance of two bows. And of course, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> always speaks in a language that the Arabs can understand. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> uses the same metaphors that they understand. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَنَا</span> actually represents a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span>. It doesn''t represent a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَكَان</span>, right? A <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَقَام</span> is a station. So it represents the closeness of station, not of place, because there is no physicality there. You cannot go into the physical presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> because he''s not physical and he''s beyond physicality.</p>
<p>Now, if you look at the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>ic description of this experience that he had in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مِعْرَاج</span>, it says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا زَاغَ ٱلْبَصَرُ وَمَا طَغَىٰ</span> ‎. His vision did not deviate, nor did it rebel against him. Now, we need to understand this in the context of what was happening to him. Imagine, I want you to imagine as I say this, that he''s moving through the seven heavens, which means that he was being given this unveiling of the unseen world, this numenal realm. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالِمُ الْغَيْبِ</span>, the unseen world, was literally being unveiled for him. Now if we all had that same experience of this unveiling right now, right, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> removed this veil from before us, the vast majority of us would just literally go insane. We''d just not be able to handle it. Because it would be too much stimulation, right?</p>
<p>We know in our material sciences today, we know that we''re seeing less than a billionth of the material stimuli at any given moment. In other words, when you and I look out into the room we''re sitting in right now, we are seeing less than a billionth of what is materially there to stimulate our senses. Visible light, it''s a very small spectrum on the electromagnetic spectrum. I mean, and that''s how limited we are if we have 20/20 vision. So, and then we also know that the majority of the universe is dark matter. We know that, right?</p>
<p>95% of the physical universe is not even perceived. So when these scientists, when they started looking outside, they saw all these stars everywhere. So they had to ask the question, why isn''t the night sky as bright as the day sky? Because if light travels and if all of these enormous lights are out there, right? If the heavens is filled with these orbs of light, then why aren''t they filling up the night sky the same way? Cause it should be like really bright all the time. Because we only see about 3,000 stars on a really clear, like a moonless night. And what they found out is that only the light from certain stars are penetrating through. It''s literally a visitation. Like they''re visiting and then returning back. And that''s really interesting because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> says, He says, I swear by the night and the night visitor.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>, 1400 years back, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is saying that the light from the stars make a journey to get here. And it''s quite literally like a guest who comes in the night. And then it says, . What will convey to you what this night visitor is? The piercing star. Now, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُقْب</span> in Arabic, it means to put a hole through a pearl. That''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُقْب</span> means. You know, when you make a pearl <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَسْبِيح</span>, you have to literally make a hole in each pearl so that you can penetrate the thread through it, right? That''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُقْب</span>, to put a hole through a pearl. And this light of the stars that we know today, they''re reaching us, they''re literally penetrating and making a hole in this celestial pearl. Isn''t that fascinating? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>.</p>
<p>Anyway, I was saying that what we know about this seen world is incredibly small, and yet the unseen world we have absolutely no knowledge of. But then, as he''s on about this journey, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says His vision did not deviate nor did it rebel against him. My question is, why not? Why did it not deviate? I mean, if you''re traveling through this unseen realm for the first time and all of this unveiling was occurring and you were witnessing these extraordinary things, why would you not be distracted? Right?</p>
<p>The answer goes back to the heart of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, who had only one concern that night, which was to visit his Lord. To meet his Lord. He was on a journey to meet his beloved, and he was not interested in the particulars, no matter how amazing they were. And so he was not distracted by anything else, because to him this was the journey of a lover to meet his beloved. I mean, if you''ve ever had that experience, you know you''re not going to care about anything else in the world. All you want is to see the face of your beloved. You''ll travel continents, fly across oceans to do it. Because they say, everything that you can ever desire exists in your beloved.</p>
<p>Your beloved is the star, the moon, the universe, right? And so the actual things don''t really matter. And this holds true in the worldly sense for us human beings. But here we''re talking about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, so you can only imagine, right? His heart''s desire was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and nothing else. And to be honest about it, if you think about, you know, everything that we are running and chasing in this world, everything we''re looking for, searching for, it''s ultimately our heart''s desire. That''s all what this is about.</p>
<p>So now when he returns from that divine experience, he''s completely transformed. He is now ready to move on to the next phase, which is to be in this world free of all attachments. Because the world is no longer a threat to him as a distraction. He has seen the ultimate reality. So now he can never misperceive this world.</p>
<p>This, in our tradition, is what''s called the right understanding or the first understanding. That we must perceive the world correctly, because our lack of perception, our misinterpretation of this world is what causes pain. And this concept is actually very dominant in the Buddhist tradition, that, you know, all suffering comes from a lack of understanding of the true nature of things. Suffering is related to desire. Now, what I find really fascinating is that the word for desire in Arabic, which is , it also means oppression. That''s awesome. The word for desire and the word for oppression are the same in Arabic. Why? Because desires oppress. So the concept of freedom in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is not the elimination of desire. It is not the elimination of desire. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> does not ask us to eliminate desire, because that''s impossible.</p>
<p>Right? What <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> teaches us is to make our desire the right desire. To desire the right things. And for a believer, there is nothing more desirable than the company of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. So these are the two incredible experiences of our Prophet, right? The experience of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَحْيٌ</span> through the medium of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span>, and a second experience, which is a direct witnessing of his master without any veils, where he receives this gift of this immense expansion in his soul, taking him to the pinnacle of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> did not begin that way for him, did it? It did not begin with a direct experience. It began with an intermediary one, through a learning process through <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span>. It began with an extreme constriction of the heart, with fear, with doubts, physical pain, of absorbing the revelation. And then followed by 13 years of persecution, trials, hardships, only then the unveiling of the unseen realm occurred for him.</p>
<p>Right? And the reason I wanted to share this with you because this had really like an incredible impact on me, so I just thought, you know, I wanted to share this with you so that, you know, especially my young, the young adults in this group, I have my nieces, my nephews, and I wanted to share this because to me, this felt like the Prophet''s experience, for me, it''s like an archetype. It''s like a precedent. Because for me, this is generally the experience of entering <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. It''s a journey for us. We are all on a path traveling towards our master. And the experience of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> sets this precedence for us. You know, just as the journey for him began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَخْلِيَة</span>, an emptying of the heart, just as it began with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَحَنُّث</span>, the identification and the detachment from elements of this world that distract us from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, we have to go through the same exercise. We have to work on managing our desires, not eliminating it, managing it, channeling it, making our desires the right desire, which is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, right? And we are in the process of the first journey, where we are learning, the noumenal realm is being unveiled for us through intermediaries, through our teachers, through our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَنَاصِب</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاءٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ائِمَّة</span>.</p>
<p>These are all our intermediaries. We''re going through the same process. For him it was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِبْرِيل</span>, for us it is them. Because we can''t really, we can''t absorb that revelation directly. So it has to be filtered. Right? But this is where it all began. And that''s what <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is, at the end of the day that''s what this is. Do you want to make this journey or not? I mean, if you''re not interested in this journey, then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is not for you. Seriously. Enjoy the pleasures of this world and actually make the most of it while you can.</p>
<p>That''s my advice to you. If you''re not interested in taking this journey to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, enjoy the world. Because it''s not gonna last. Before you know it, it''s gonna be over and you''re gonna be left in misery and despair. These are principles. We''re not making this up. This is true. You can see this, right? So first decide what you want to do. I mean, when you go on vacations, holidays, what''s the first thing you do? You look it up, right? You Google those vacation spots.</p>
<p>Why do you do that? To find out if it''s worth your effort. Right? If you''re going to spend so much money, make the effort to travel all the way across continents. You want to make sure that you''ll get what you''re looking for. Right? You ask people, have you been there? How was it? Did you have a really good time? Was it a good experience? Was it relaxing? Did you have fun?</p>
<p>Was it beautiful? Can you show me pictures? Right? Same thing here. Look it up. Look up <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, learn about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. Find out what it offers in the end, and then decide whether you want it or not. And if you don''t, no problem. Tell them that the truth has been revealed by your master. Whoever desires it can become a believer. Who does not desire it, he can reject it. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> saying in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is not for everyone. It really isn''t. I mean, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is an enormous gift that has to be earned. The company of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, the company of the Prophet is an incredible privilege that has to be earned. This is not a cheap religion. This is not an easy religion in terms of work.</p>
<p>It requires hard work. I mean, if you don''t believe me, study the life of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, then look at the end of his journey, right? Like I said, happiness is the sum total of your life. That''s felicity. It''s not the means, it''s the end. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا عَلِيٌّ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> says in one of his sermons in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَهْجُ الْبَلَاغَةِ</span>, he says, and I''m paraphrasing, but he says something like, if you are looking for glory and greatness, look at the life of the Prophet. Did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> elevate him in this world for his hard work, or did he abase him? If you say he abased him, then you are a liar. But if you say that he gave him glory, then you have spoken the truth, for his name and his efforts will shine on the sky of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> like the sun till the end of time. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>. Like, I don''t know, can''t say it in any better words. His example is right here. Look who he is, who he became and who he has become to the world, the love for him. But it wasn''t easy.</p>
<p>So you make a decision. Have you guys watched that Matrix? It''s literally blue pill or red pill. You can take the blue pill and you can go back to sleep and enjoy the delusions and shadows of this world, right? Or you can take the red pill and wake up. Disconnect from the Matrix. I mean, it''s a choice. Now, if you do decide to take the red pill, even in that movie, right, Keanu Reeves, what does he have to do once you take the red pill? It''s all work, training. So if you decide that you want to take this journey, then prepare for it. Don''t be afraid of hard work.</p>
<p>Yes, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is a lot of hard work. So what? Look at a bodybuilder, right? Look at these incredible athletes. I mean, you think they got where they are today just by wanting to get there? No. Hard work, discipline, endless hours of training, proper diet, millions of reps and sets consistently done year after year. But go and ask them if it was worth it, see what they say. They''ll say it was absolutely worth it. So if you want this path of spiritual ascension, then focus. His vision did not deviate. It did not rebel against him.</p>
<p>Don''t let the world distract you. Don''t let the temptations get you off your path. And we will make it. I mean, I know what I want. I made my choice a long time ago. To me personally, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> was really difficult when I started, but the more I learned about it, the more I practiced it, the easier it got for me. And I''m sure many in this group, we have elders, my parents are in here, they''ll testify that it''s true to be as their own experience. I love my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> today. I love the experience of my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. I''m not just saying that, you know that I do. Many of you know me personally, right? It has transformed me completely.</p>
<p>I mean the contentment, the freedom that I feel. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. My <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَّاصٌ</span> are actually in this group. I have at least three <span class="ar-inline" d' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'islam as an experience';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلَّاصٌ</span> in here and others from my family. Ask them, seriously. They''ll tell you who I used to be compared to who I am today. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>. And yes, I''ll make mistakes. I''ll still continue to make mistakes along the way. So what? Who has asked you for perfection? Show me one verse in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> that demands perfection. One verse. Over 6000 verses in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, show me one verse that says you have to be perfect. No, never. All it demands is effort, right? All I want from this life is to meet my Lord, my Prophet as a better and improved version of from when I came in this world. That''s all. I''m never going to be perfect. I''m never gonna not miss my prayer. I''m never gonna not miss a fast. I will sin from time to time. So what? I''m not God. I''m human. Only God is perfect. What, bodybuilders, they don''t miss a training day? They don''t have cheat days? Athletes don''t make mistakes? So what, do they stop training? Do they stop playing?</p>
<p>Did they start blaming the game? I mean, that''s ridiculous, right? Anyway, I just wanted to, you know, honestly share these experiences with you because they really helped me make a choice. You know, I really clearly know what I want and I know what I have to do to get where I wanted to get and, you know, what I''ll have to give up for it, and I''m cool with that. I have no regrets about it.</p>
<p>And this amazing experience of contentment, of just freedom, is something you can have also. So may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> help us all follow in the footsteps of our Prophet, and may He enable us to truly, truly experience the beauty of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>, because it is so beautiful. I can''t, I mean... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, I can''t, you know, there''s my grandfather, used to, my mother used to say that, he said that, you know, if you knew how lucky we are to be Muslims, we would never raise our head from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَجْدَة</span>. And I never got that. I''m like, that is such an extreme exaggeration.</p>
<p>Like, what does that even mean? But today, I know what that means. I honestly, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, I know what it means. I cannot, I cannot even express how grateful I am for this guidance that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has blessed me with. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ، رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِ مُحَمَّدٍ الْخَاتِمِ عَلَيْهِمْ أَجْمَعِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا إِنَّنَا سَمِعْنَا مُنَادِيًا يُنَادِي لِلْإِيمَانِ أَنْ آمِنُوا بِرَبِّكُمْ فَآمَنَّا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا فَاغْفِرْ لَنَا ذُنُوبَنَا وَكَفِّرْ عَنَّا سَيِّئَاتِنَا وَتَوَفَّنَا مَعَ الْأَبْرَارِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبَّنَا وَآتِنَا مَا وَعَدْتَنَا عَلَى رُسُلِكَ</span></p>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'islam as an experience';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ صَانِعِ الْخَلْقِ عَلَى أَتَمِّ نِظَامٍ وَأَحْسَنِ قِوَامٍ الْوَاضِعِ عَلَى أَشْرَفِ أَجْدَرِ مَقَامٍ وَأَرْفَقِ مَقَامٍ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ عَدَدَ خَلْقِهِ وَزِنَةَ عَرْشِهِ وَمِدَادَ كَلِمَاتِهِ وَمُنْتَهَى عِلْمِهِ وَجَمِيعَ مَا شَاءَ وَخَلَقَ وَبَرَأَ وَهُوَ عَالِمُ الْغَيْبِ وَالشَّهَادَةِ الرَّحْمَنُ الرَّحِيمُ الْمَلِكُ الْقُدُّوسُ الْعَزِيزُ الْحَكِيمُ وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى الْمُؤَيَّدِ أَبْلَغِ الْكَلَامِ مُحَمَّدٍ الْمَبْعُوثِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ لِهِدَايَةِ كَافَّةِ الْأَنَامِ إِلَى سُبُلِ السَّلَامِ وَإِلَى مَدَلَّةِ اللَّيَالِي وَالْأَيَّامِ الَّذِي أَرْسَلَهُ اللهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَى وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَى بِاللهِ شَهِيدًا وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُهُ وَرَسُولُهُ وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مَوْلَانَا عَلِيًّا وَصِيُّهُ وَوَزِيرُهُ فَصَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَعَلَى آلِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَسَلِّمْ عَلَيْهِمْ تَسْلِيمًا كَثِيرًا كَثِيرًا اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا اللَّهُمَّ آتِ نُفُوسَنَا تَقْوَاهَا وَزَكِّهَا فَأَنْتَ خَيْرُ مَنْ زَكَّاهَا أَنْتَ وَلِيُّنَا وَمَوْلَانَا وَنِعْمَ الْمَوْلَى وَنِعْمَ النَّصِيرُ وَلَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِاللهِ الْعَلِيِّ الْعَظِيمِ فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ يَقُولُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ بَعْدَ أَقُولُ أَعُوذُ بِاللهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمَنِ الرَّحِيمِ رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَمَاتِ عَلَى لَا إِلَهَ إِلَّا اللهُ وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span></p>
<p>Once again, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, today I <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve the honor of speaking about the beloved of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. He was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبِيبُ اللهِ</span>, the beloved of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. His name is Mu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>mmad. The name Mu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>mmad comes from the root word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْد</span>, which means praise and gratitude. And Mu<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>mmad is the one who is constantly praised and who is worthy of eternal gratitude. And so, just as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>, the master of everything in existence, His messenger is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةً لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span>.</p>
<p>He is a mercy for everything in existence. So everything t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبّ</span> of, He is a mercy for. And He is the one whose love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s made a prerequisite for His own love, in t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t we cannot claim the love of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> without loving His beloved Messenger, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>.</p>
<p>Scholars say t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَحَبَّة</span>, or love, for most human beings, it arises out of three things. The first, they say, is physical love. You see something beautiful, your heart inclines towards them, right? Your eyes delight in it. If you see a human being t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is physically beautiful, you can fall in love. It can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppen just from sight. And the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُلَمَاء حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve maintained the physical description of our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, which is the description of a very <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ndsome man.</p>
<p>The second type of love comes from when you hear about the beautiful qualities of a person. So there is outward beauty, and then there is inward beauty, which is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t I <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve the honor of s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ring with you today.</p>
<p>The third type of love, they say, is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you feel for someone who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s done good for you, or t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you find out t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t somebody made a sacrifice for you. So when someone does something good to a decent human being, they love them more and more for t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t reason. But when good is done to an ingrate, he resents t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t person because it generates an envy in his heart. They begin to feel indebted and they do not like t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t feeling of being indebted. So they begin to use strategies within themselves to not feel indebted to t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t person, and these are sick people. This is why the hypocrites, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُنَافِقُونَ</span>, are the sickest people, and they are rightfully in the lowest place in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Because human hearts by nature love those who do good to them. If somebody does good for you and then you do not <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve love for them in return, it''s because the heart is not in a natural state, it''s in a diseased state. And if you knew w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the Prophet did for you, you would just naturally fall in love with him. And he did not just do it for you or for, you know, certain people.</p>
<p>He did it for the entire humanity. His concern was not just for his companions or his family. You know, most human beings, we''re just concerned, you know, we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve limited concerns. Our concern is limited to wife, children, husband, family, at the most extended family, right? Some people, their concern is the community. They care about the poor people in the community. They want to find them and help them, because ultimately the love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s to do with the heart. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s where love resides, right? The bigger the heart, the more capacity for love, and the smaller the heart, the lower the capacity for love. And our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d the heart t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was concerned for the entire humanity, for generations to come. He was concerned for animals. In the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شَرِيعَة</span>, he gave us guidelines on how to treat animals with kindness. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>.</p>
<p>Learning about the Prophet Seera, his life story, is one of the most beautiful things there is to learn. And this information is readily available in books and on the internet. I mean, you can look t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t up. I would love to speak about it, but for the want of time, I''m just limiting my session to just talk about some of the salient features of his life and s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>re with you w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t an incredible man he was. And so I want to focus today''s session on <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>, if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> gives me an opportunity, someday I would love to just do a session on his physical description, which is quite incredible.</p>
<p>Anyway, so today I want to focus this talk on his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span> comes from the root word , which means to create. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْقٌ</span>, He is the creator, and we are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخْلُوق</span>, we are the creation. We are... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْقٌ</span>, He is the creator, and we are the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَخْلُوق</span>, we are the created beings. We are comprised of a body and a spirit t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t animates t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t body, right?</p>
<p>Our material form, the material form of our body is the . And the spiritual form of our soul is the . It''s the same three letters. The body is nourished by the physical world, which is food and drink. Our spirits are nourished by knowledge, by c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, which is the plural form of the word . So, the or the represents our physical existence, whereas represents our spiritual existence. And they both begin with the letter . Interestingly, the letter in Arabic is a sound of friction. It''s a sound, they say it''s a sound of effort, even when you articulate it, right? So if you look at any word in the Arabic language t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t begins with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>, you''ll see it is either <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rsh in its nature or it requires effort. So for instance, , which is service, servitude, right? begins with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> and it requires effort.</p>
<figure class="md-figure"><img src="/media/love-of-the-prophet/image/213/447012f0-26e8-4372-8644-f4d5a20d6582.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<p>Friendship requires effort. Fear, it''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rsh emotion. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَيَالٌ</span>, imagination, it requires effort to build. So you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْق</span> and you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُقٌ</span>, both starting with the letter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>. Why? Because the body takes effort to develop and so does c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. C<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter takes a lot of effort to develop. When we die, our fully developed body returns back into the earth and our soul with its fully developed c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter returns back to the Creator, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلْقٌ</span>. So c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter is the real earning and the real acquisition of the soul. A soul t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t returns back to its master without a developed c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter is an impoverished soul. When the body is weak, it cannot benefit from the offerings of this world. And when a soul and a spirit is weak, when it lacks c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, it is unable to enjoy the pleasures of the afterlife.</p>
<p>Islam, in its essence, is composed of two parts: worship and the development of c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرِبَ بِهَا</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. This is why our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> was sent to teach us and perfect our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was his mission. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا بُعِثْتُ لِأُتَمِّمَ مَكَارِمَ الْأَخْلَاقِ</span>. I was sent for no other reason. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا</span> is a very special usage in the Arabic language. It emp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>sizes t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>tever is being said, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s the only reason. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا بُعِثْتُ لِأُتَمِّمَ مَكَارِمَ الْأَخْلَاقِ</span>. I was sent for nothing else but to perfect human c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter.</p>
<p>Now, if you ask a Muslim w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was the Prophet''s miracle, the unanimous answer t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you will get is always the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, right? The gift and the miracle indeed to the Arab was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, because the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَمْ</span> cannot understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. The word in Arabic for a non-Arab is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَمْ</span>, which means dumb or mute. Somebody who can''t speak, like for instance, the Arabs say, right, the dumb animal. And this is not just limited to Arabic, this applies to other languages as well. For instance, the Greeks, right? The word for a non-Greek for them was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْبَر</span>, from which we get <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْبَر</span>ian. So for in Greek <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرْبَر</span> means the one who cannot speak. There''s similar, there''s a word for non-Zulu, right? They <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve the same thing. So people, when they look at other people of other languages and cannot understand them, they assume it must be like animals, because, you know, we can''t understand the speech of animals.</p>
<p>But the Arabs, when they considered non-Arabs as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَمْ</span>, as mute people, it was not out of an insult. They <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d a very good reason for t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. Because they would go to a culture, for instance, they would go to a Persian and they would ask, How many words do you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve for a lion?</p>
<p>And the Persians would say, &quot;We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve one word, lion.&quot; W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>tever in their language the word was for lion. They would say, &quot;We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve one word for lion.&quot; And they would say, &quot;You <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve one word for lion? We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve over 350 words just for lion.&quot; There are over 350 words in the ancient Arabic just for lion. They would say, &quot;We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve , which is a lion when it hunts at night. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve , which is a short, strong lion. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَسْوَرَة</span>, which is a lion when the animals are fleeing from it. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْث</span>, which is a lion when, you know, they say ... when a lion is circling its prey to attack it, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيْث</span>.&quot; They say, &quot;We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُسْوَةٌ حَسَنَةٌ</span>, which is a lion when it''s leaping to attack.&quot;</p>
<p>So all of these words t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the Arabs use just for a lion. Then there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَمْزَة</span>, which is the lion when he''s roaring. There is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شِبْلٌ</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَرَدَ</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَبَّاس</span>, which is used for the leader of the pack. There''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>, there''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَصَلٌ</span>, there is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">درَامٌ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِبَال</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَيَّاس</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَضَنْفَر</span>. So many names just for a lion. So when they would hear the Persian say t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve only one word for a lion, right, to them they were like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَمّ</span>, mute, dumb. So this is the way they would look at things.</p>
<p>Now, if you sent a message to people and it''s in Arabic, and the miracle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is truly Arabic, there''s no doubt about t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, right? The true <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجْزَلُ الْقُرْآنِ</span>, the true miracle of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is absolutely in the linguistic perfection of Arabic. And I did, I think, over 10 sessions on this a few years ago, looking at the miracle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> from 10 different angles, linguistic angles. So Arabic is, no doubt about it, an incredible language. Absolutely no doubt about it. Ancient Arabic, not today''s Arabic, not the dialectical Arabic, the original ancient Arabic. But w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t does the non-Arab <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve? How does the non-Arab come to know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t Islam is indeed a revelation from God? When we read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> in English or when we read it in Urdu, we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve a difficult time understanding it because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is not a linear book, right?</p>
<p>In the Bible, the Bible, it starts with &quot;in the beginning,&quot; right? Bible starts with &quot;in the beginning.&quot; T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s how the Bible starts, &quot;in the beginning.&quot; The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> starts with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>. W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t does t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t mean, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الم</span>? We don''t know w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t means. And then it says, &quot;T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is the book in which there is no doubt.&quot; Not &quot;this is the book in which there is no doubt,&quot; &quot;t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is the book.&quot; So this is how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> begins his book after <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْفَاتِحَة</span>.</p>
<p>So for us as non-Arabs, it gets very confusing because these things can only be understood properly through Arabic linguistics or the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طَوِيل</span> or things like t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. But how does the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَمْ</span>, the non-Arab, come to understand these things? How does the non-Arab know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this is the truth? And the answer is, look at how they became Muslim. Wherever they went, wherever the Arabs went, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was the factor, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t deciding factor t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t led to for these non-Arabs to become Muslim? It wasn''t the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْجَازُ الْقُرْآنِ</span>, because they couldn''t understand Arabic. It was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْجَازُ الْأَخْلَاقِ</span>, it was the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَجَزُّ الْخُلْقِ</span>, the miracle of c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. This was w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t struck the non-Arab''s heart. And this is why the gift t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> gave to the non-Arabs, to us, is the messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. This is why we see t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t all over the non-Arab world we find this immense love in the hearts of people for the messenger. Why? Because of his beautiful c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, because of his high ethical standards.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, I think it''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ النُّونِ وَالْقَلَمِ</span>, he begins t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t surah, I think, with the verse in which he says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says to his messenger, absolutely without a doubt you are committed to a high ethical standard, a great ethical standard. W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t many people don''t realize is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this particular verse, it was revealed at a very early stage, right after he was chosen as prophet. This verse was revealed right at the beginning of his mission. And w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t suggests is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this verse is not referring to the Prophet''s c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter as a prophet, it''s referring to his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter as a person, as a member of t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t society for 40 years, because there is yet the 23 years of his prophethood to unfold. It <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>sn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppened yet.</p>
<p>This verse is coming right at the beginning, which is showing t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this praise is for his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter as a person, not as a prophet. It''s referring to his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter pre-Islam. So he is this regular citizen of this community when God is praising his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. Why? You know, because you can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve great things to say, you know, as a preacher, even as a prophet, but a message is hollow in and of itself if it''s not fortified by credibility. And one of the most sustainable forms of credibility is c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. I mean, today you can <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve so many eloquent, well-spoken politicians, right? We see t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t all the time. But w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s the easiest way to dismantle their entire political campaign? Just smear their c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, right? If you can just find something t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s a flaw in their c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, some kind of corruption, then it doesn''t matter how well they speak or how highly they speak of ideals. None of it really matters at t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t point because there''s no c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter to back up those high claims.</p>
<p>And so w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s really incredible about this verse is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is praising his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter right at the beginning. Because Makkah pre-Islam was a horrible society. We know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, right? Makkah was, like, pretty gangster at t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t time. They were corrupt in their business practices. They were corrupt in their social practices. There was inequality, injustice, law of the jungle. The strong would beat up the weak just because they could, might was right. This was the kind of society, this was the kind of lifestyle t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d. And they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d great economic promise and they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d great economic strength. So t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s why they didn''t feel the need to reform themselves. Now, when there is one decent person living in an overwhelmingly corrupt society who sticks to his honesty, who sticks to his principle, how difficult do you think it is for t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t person to survive?</p>
<p>And yet, not only does he survive, he stands out so much from the rest. Not because he was preaching anything. As a matter of fact, if you read his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>, he''s described as a quiet individual. He was the quiet type originally. But because of his business dealings with people, because of his honesty, because of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, he got named <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّادِقُ وَالْأَمِينُ</span>. In a culture where poetry was used to insult people, in t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t culture, he earned the title <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الصَّادِقُ وَالْأَمِينُ</span>, the truthful one and the trustworthy one. And so in this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, he refers to t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t and he says to the people of Makkah, you''re questioning the integrity of this man as a messenger? When you''ve never questioned his integrity for 40 years as the most decent among you? You who don''t like to praise anyone, you who are obsessed with praising only yourselves, you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve praised him. So the ultimate testimony of his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t even those who didn''t really want to accept his message, they couldn''t deny the vastness and the greatness of his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> describes his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَةً لِلْعَالَمِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَكَ إِلَّا رَحْمَةًۭ لِّلْعَلَمِينَ</span>. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve only sent you as a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>, as a mercy to all mankind. He was sent to honor human beings. In this verse, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> is describing him as mercy. He''s calling him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحْمَة</span>. He''s not saying t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the Prophet is merciful. He''s not saying he''s someone manifesting an attribute. No, he''s saying he is the attribute himself. Because he was extremely kind and extremely gentle by nature. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا</span> Ali in one of his descriptions about the Prophet said he was the gentlest of people by disposition. His <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلُق</span> was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيِّنٌ</span>.</p>
<p>He was soft by his nature. And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْمُؤْمِنُ هَيِّنٌ وَلَيِّنٌ</span>. The believer is soft and gentle by his nature. When he was described by one of his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, he said, you know, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he didn''t make things difficult for people. Nor did he go out seeking faults in people, but instead he taught people and he made things easy for them. So part of his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> was t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was sent for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَيْسِيرٌ</span>, for ease to human beings. He was sent to make our lives easy. He was sent to take out the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخِلَّاء</span>, the s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ckles and the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ins of previous dispensations. He came to set people free of the s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ckles of their own lower selves, of their desire, because desires oppress. So he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بُعِثْتُ بِالْحَنِيفِيَّةِ السَّمْحَةِ</span>, I was sent with the forgiving and gentle <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَنَفِيَّة</span> way. The Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve mercy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَحِمَ اللَّهُ امْرَأً سَمْحًا إِذَا بَاعَ وَإِذَا اشْتَرَى</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve mercy on a man who is forgiving when he is buying or selling.</p>
<p>You know, he prayed for mercy for people like t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. You know, those who when they buy and they sell, they say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّةٌ</span>, you know, if the man <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s 10 <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِرْهَمٌ</span> and the item costs 12, take it. Don''t worry about it. Don''t worry about the two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِرْهَمٌ</span>. Or for the man buying the item, if the merc<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>nt doesn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>nge, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خُلَّةٌ</span>, take the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِرْهَم</span>. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَمَاحَة</span>. So he taught people how to be forgiving. I was sent with the tolerant way of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَنِيف</span>. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَنِيف</span> is the person who inclines towards the truth or somebody who inclines towards righteousness. So he is the greatest manifestation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>''s mercy to humanity. And one of the greatest gifts t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t our Prophet gave us is his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. His greatest gift to us is not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَحْكَام</span>.</p>
<p>It''s not the injunctions, but how he lived those injunctions, how he carried out those injunctions. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t it is from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he made you a mercy, and if you were <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rd-hearted, right? T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t if you were <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rsh, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَضْل</span>, you know, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَزَعٌ</span> means <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rshness in the self. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> says if you were <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَضْل</span>, or <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَلِيظَ الْقَلْبِ</span>, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَلِيظٌ</span> comes from the root <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غِزّ</span>, which means heaviness. So if your heart was heavy, if it was weighty, then people would just disperse from around you. And by nature, we do not like people to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rsh with us. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s just a natural thing, right? Aristotle said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the root cause of all enmity among human beings is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t somebody feels slighted by another person. The sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِحْنَة</span>, of being insulted. But when it came to the Prophet, they said his gathering was like, he gave everyone in his companions a due. He fulfilled the due if they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d a due coming. He gave everybody his complete attention.</p>
<p>So much so t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t each one of them thought t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was the most important one in t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t gathering. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَى مُحَمَّدٍ وَآلِهِ</span>. I mean, how amazing is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t? He was constantly gathered by companions and they say every companion says t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he made them feel t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was the most important, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was the most beloved. Can you imagine how difficult t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is to do? We go out in groups, right? Can we do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t? He always preferred the best for the other person over him. Always, there''s a narration in which he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِوَاكَ</span>, right? The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِوَاكَ</span> is the stick t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is used for brushing teeth.</p>
<p>Even today in Middle East and Arab countries it''s used. So, you know, he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d two <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِوَاكَ</span>, two sticks, and there was an <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعْرَابِيّ</span>, a Bedouin there. And one of the sticks t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d was broken. It <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d a crookedness in it. And the other one was straight. So he looked at the two and then he gave the Bedouin the straight one and he kept the crooked one for himself. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيثَار</span>, altruism. And one of the scholars said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t if this is his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيثَار</span> for a simple Bedouin, who is a stranger and who doesn''t even care if it''s crooked or straight, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t about the rest of the people t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he actually, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, you know, the people closest to the Prophet. So this was his nature, always deciding the better, the best for the other person. We''re the other way around. I''ll keep the best and give you the one, you know, I''ll regift something to you. I''m not gonna get you a gift t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t I would get for myself.</p>
<p>I''m just gonna save all the other gifts t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t people gave me and I''m just gonna wrap them up and give it to you. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s me. But the Prophet always preferred to give the better gift to the other person. He was gentle in his teachings. You know, one of them said to the Prophet, this man, he was in prayer and somebody sneezed during prayer. And at t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t point, keep in mind t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t these injunctions, they were still coming down and they all didn''t know everything clearly. So there''s this gathering, people are doing prayers and somebody sneezed. And this man who was in prayer, during the prayer, he said, may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve mercy on you, which is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t we say when we hear somebody sneeze in Islam, right? But the thing is he did t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t during the prayer and everybody lost it in the prayer, which is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you would expect today anywhere, right? Are you crazy? So they all started striking their fist on their thighs, right?</p>
<p>Like this, trying to shut them up. And so this poor man, he realized he did something wrong and he just became quiet. He says, after the prayer, the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he called him, &quot;Come over.&quot; And the man says, &quot;I got really scared. I thought he was gonna get angry with me because t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was the Arab nature. I thought he was gonna beat me. He''s gonna punish me.&quot; So he said, he went to the Prophet all scared. And the Prophet said to him, no, he said, he narrates this. He says, &quot;When I went to the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ما كَهَرَنِي، وَلَا ضَرَبَنِي، وَلَا شَتَمَنِي</span>. He didn''t look at me in a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>rsh way.</p>
<p>He didn''t say anything mean to me. He didn''t strike me or hit me. Instead, he said to me, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ هَذِهِ الصَّلَاةَ</span>. Look, this is our prayer. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَا يَصْلُحُ فِيهَا شَيْءٌ مِنْ كَلَامِ النَّاسِ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ مَاهِيَّةَ تَسْبِيحٌ وَتَهْدِيدٌ وَتَكْبِيرٌ</span>.&quot; He said this is our prayer to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and it is not right for us to talk amongst ourselves during our prayer because we''re speaking to God. Prayer is meant to praise the Lord, to remember him, to elevate his name. I mean, how do we be<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve? I mean, this is the sad part, right? When you look into this mirror, so we don''t look good, do we? The man said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللهِ يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>.&quot;</p>
<p>He said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve never seen anyone teach like this to me before or after.&quot; Look how we teach our children, right? Scolding, yelling, beating. He was gentle, he was loving in his teaching mec<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>nisms.</p>
<p>You know, one man came to the Prophet. He said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, everything in Islam makes sense to me except for one thing.&quot; He said, &quot;I love women.&quot; He loved women. He wanted to go and sleep with whoever he wanted. So he said, you know, he came to the Prophet, he said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I want to become Muslim, but I love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span>. I love fornication.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>.</p>
<p>Go and try saying this to anyone in authority today. I c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>llenge you, go and try to say this, go to try anybody in authority, go and tell them you love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span> and see w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he says. Can you even imagine t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t? This is the Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. This man is saying this to no other t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللَّهِ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. So he said to the Prophet, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زِينَة</span>.&quot; The Prophet said to him, &quot;Do you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve a sister?&quot; He said, &quot;Yes.&quot; He said, &quot;Would you like somebody to go and sleep with your sister?&quot; He said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَشَا</span>, never.&quot; He said, &quot;Do you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" l' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'character of the prophet';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || 'ang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve an aunt?&quot; He said, &quot;Yes.&quot; He said, &quot;Would you like somebody to sleep with your aunt without <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبّ</span>?&quot; He said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَشَا</span>, never.&quot; &quot;Do you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve a mother?&quot; He said, &quot;Yes.&quot; &quot;Would you like somebody to do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t to your mother?&quot;</p>
<p>The man said, &quot;No.&quot; He said, &quot;The woman t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you want to sleep with, they are either somebody''s sister or somebody''s aunt or somebody''s mother. Is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t right for you to put them through t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t for your own pleasures?&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, the man said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّهِ</span>, I went in the company of the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> with a love for sleeping with women more t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n anything without taking the responsibility.&quot; He said, &quot;I walked out of there and it was the most detestable thing to me.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. This was his way in c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>nging people. This is how he transformed people. The most, the worst. Today we ask a question t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is inappropriate. It''s like, how dare you? You''re going to hell.</p>
<p>S<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>me on you. We make people feel miserable for just a question they want to know about. He told jokes. You know, his wives in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سِيرَة</span>, they''ve said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was always joking with us in the house. He was lighthearted. You know, the Prophet, he said, &quot;I joke, but I never tell lies in my joke. I always speak the truth.&quot; A man came to the Prophet and he said, &quot;Let me borrow a camel.&quot; And the Prophet said, &quot;I''ll give you the baby of a she-camel.&quot; And he said, &quot;W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t would t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t benefit me?&quot; Right? A baby of a she-camel, because he wanted to ride it. Prophet said, &quot;Isn''t every camel the baby of a she-camel?&quot; Right? Just lighthearted conversation, but truth. Another time an old lady came up to him and said, &quot;Am I going to go to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جَنَّةٌ</span>?&quot; She was an old lady. He said, he said, &quot;Old women do not go to paradise.&quot;</p>
<p>And she became sad. And then he laughed and he said, you will go in young and beautiful. And then she was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppy. He was a merciful man. He was a forgiving man.</p>
<p>A man came to the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> and said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَالَتُ حَالَتُ</span>, O messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, I <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve destroyed myself. He said, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t did you do? He says, I slept with my wife in the daytime in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> said, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t made you do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t? He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, he said, you know, I saw my wife in the light of the moon, in the moonlight, and I just couldn''t help myself. He laughed at the man. He said, don''t worry, go do the expiation, go free a slave. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I don''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve any possessions. Okay, no problem, go fast two months. The man said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I can''t do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. I mean, in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span> I''m <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ving trouble. He said, I can''t even do the fast of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَمَضَان</span>, you''re telling me to do two months? He said, no problem, get some dates, go feed 60 people. He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, I don''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve any money, I <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve nothing to buy dates with. He told somebody, bring some dates. He gave those dates to t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t man. He said, go give it to people. The man said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, do they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve to be poor? He said, yes, they <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve to be <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فُقَرَاء</span>.</p>
<p>He said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا رَسُولَ اللهِ</span>, no one''s more poor t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n me and my own family in my tribe. So he said, go and eat it with your family. Go feed your family. Ease. He was just constantly making things easy for people. This was his nature. This was his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter. You know, even when people came to him full of sin, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُذْنِبُونَ</span>, this man, he was in a state of sin. But the Prophet was showing his humanity by letting this man feel <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَابَ رَحْمَةُ اللهِ وَاسِعٌ</span>. He was just letting the man know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you''re a human being. You made a mistake. It''s okay.</p>
<p>T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he did with people. He made them realize t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the doors of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy are wide open. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s all he ever did. I mean, this is why, you know, to make people despair from the mercy of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, these are evil people, I''m telling you, who do this. I don''t care who t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t person is. If he turns you towards despair and hopelessness in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is an evil person in my book. I don''t care how many times he prays a day. I don''t care how much he fasts. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is not the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of my prophet.</p>
<p>You know, there''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ قُدْسِيٌّ</span> in which prophet says t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, you know, whoever says t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> will not forgive you, he will send t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t person to hell. Don''t judge people. Don''t tell people t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, you know, where <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy is or where his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَرَكَة</span> is. Don''t do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, really. I mean, who do we think we are? We don''t know anything. Nothing. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve absolutely no knowledge about <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Do not elevate yourself over <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. We do not know who is going where. I don''t even know where I''m going. I certainly cannot assume where you are going or where other people are going when I don''t even know where I''m going. Right?</p>
<p>So we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve big hopes in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, no doubt about it, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy is vast and we <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve high expectations from our Lord, just as <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s high hopes with us. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve high hopes with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> on the day of judgment. And the Prophet was always calling people to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> mercy, always inviting people to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> hope.</p>
<p>The worst of people. There was this Bedouin who was a Jew, and Bedouins, if you know anything about Bedouins, they''re very rough people generally, because they''re uncultured, they live their entire lives in the desert. So there was this Jewish Bedouin who absolutely <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ted the Prophet and he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ted Islam. And he was so extreme in his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>tred t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was willing to die for it. Imagine t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. So one day the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> was sitting with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span> in his mosque and this guy walks in, he goes to a corner and he begins urinating in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span>. Keep in mind, this is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِدُ النَّبَوِيِّ</span> we were talking about, right? The second most esteemed and honored place for a Muslim after the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span>. And he knows, he knows w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the companions would do to him. He knew they were going to kill him for this.</p>
<p>And he does t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t still. So imagine the psychological state of this man, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>tred in his heart for the Prophet and Islam t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was willing to die for his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>te. W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t would you do? Seriously, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t would you do if someone walks into your living room and starts urinating? I mean, think about it, seriously. Put yourself in his shoes. How would you be<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve? So naturally, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَحَابَة</span>, they pulled out their swords. They were furious. They were ready to kill this guy for his insolent be<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>vior and for disrespecting the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> and the sanctified <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَسْجِد</span>. And the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he stopped them. He said, wait, let him finish.</p>
<p>This is the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, because you know we know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t it''s very painful to stop once you start urinating. So he said to them, wait, let him finish. And then once he was done, he asked his companions to go wash the place with water. And then he went up to this man and he said, brother, every human being needs to urinate. We all as human beings <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t need within us, but this place is not made for t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. This place is made for worship of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>.</p>
<p>He says, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t Jew, he said these kind words of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, he said, transformed my entire life. He couldn''t believe it. He was expecting to be chopped to pieces. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he wanted. And here there is this man disp<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَيِّنٌ</span>g the most extreme form of love and mercy. So the man, he says to the Prophet, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَأَشْهَدُ أَنَّكَ رَسُولُ اللَّهِ</span>, I testify t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t there is no God but <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> and you indeed are his messenger. This was our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. And then the man says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِدًى أَبِي وَأُمِّي بِكَ يَا رَسُولَ اللَّهِ</span>, may my mother and my father be ransomed for you.</p>
<p>I walked in here with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>tred in my heart for you and your religion. And now I''m going to walk out of this place with nothing but love and respect in my heart. And I, he said, I swear to you, I will not show you my inauspicious face again until I convert every single member in my tribe to Islam. This is how he taught people, with his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, with his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>. He was gentle, he was kind, he was merciful. He was always reflecting. He didn''t take rest like other people. He was always concerned about his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أُمَّة</span>, his nation. He never spoke about anything t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t was unnecessary. He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d long periods of silence. He used to open and close his words with a full expression. He spoke with comprehensive words.</p>
<p>He never said anything in excess. And he was never at a loss for words. And his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter was such t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t when he walked among people, he would just stop and he would talk. You know, he would ask people, how are you doing? He wanted to genuinely know their states. They said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t in his description, they said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t when he spoke to someone, he gave them his full face. You know, he didn''t listen to them while they were talking to his side. He would literally turn around and face them when he would listen to them. He would give them his entire being, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَرْبِيَة</span>, this is education. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s all he was doing, teaching people how to listen. He never interrupted anyone while they were speaking, no matter how long they spoke.</p>
<p>How long do we listen before we interrupt? We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve no listening skills today. We zone out, we tune out. And if we do listen, we listen to respond. We don''t listen when someone''s really talking to us, right? Most people are just preparing a response while the other person is talking. We don''t empathize. We''re just listening to give advice. So in his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter is such an amazing lesson for all of us to learn.</p>
<p>He was kind and loving to children. He once visited the house of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَنَسُ بْنُ مَالِكٍ</span>, who <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d a little brother. His name was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو عَامِر</span>. You know, his father got him a little bird t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he used to play with. They used to call it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُغَر</span>. It''s a small little bird. So once he came over and he saw <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو عَامِر</span> was sad. So he asked his parents, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s troubling him? Look at t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, right? He''s coming to visit the family, and he sees one member of the family, this child, is depressed. We go into the houses of people and we''re not even aware of their states, of people around us. We don''t read expressions. He walked in, he looked and saw t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this little boy is depressed. He asked the parents, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s troubling him?</p>
<p>They said, his little bird died. So the Prophet went up to him, sat next to him, &quot;Oh Abu Umair, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppened to your little bird?&quot; I mean, he''s a little child t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t just lost a bird. He''s the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, sent to guide humanity. Why does he need to sit with this little kid? No, he sits with him, consoles him, spends time with him till the boy is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppy. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللهِ</span>, he teaches him about death. He teaches him the lesson t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t everything in this world is going to die. And he''s in a better place. He''s with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. He makes the child <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ppy.</p>
<p>He speaks to him at his level. He loved children. He honored them. You know, once he was walking with Imam Hussain riding on his shoulder, and a man saw it and he said to Imam Hussain, he said, &quot;<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نِعْمَ الْمَرْقَبُ</span>,&quot; w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t an excellent vehicle you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve. Because he was riding the shoulder of the Prophet. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَرْقَب</span> is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you ride on. So he said, &quot;W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t an excellent riding vehicle you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve.&quot; And the Prophet said, . And he said, &quot;Also, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t an excellent rider.&quot; Look at t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, right? This is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَشْرِيف</span>, honoring the child, giving him importance. This is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the Prophet did. We''re like, &quot;Shut up.</p>
<p>Don''t interrupt while we''re talking, adults talking here, get lost.&quot; <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, he made everyone feel important. He gave them importance. This is nurturing the soul. You know, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, he looked at the soul. He looked at everyone as a human being. Look how we treat our servants, people of less stature, right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> always saw human beings. He didn''t see race, he didn''t see creed, he didn''t see religion, he didn''t see color. He just saw human beings, because for him every human being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s importance. In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> eyes, no one is insignificant. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَوْلَانَا</span> Ali, he says, w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t?</p>
<p>T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t famous <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دَوَاؤُكَ فِيكَ وَمَا تَشْعُرُ، وَدَاؤُكَ مِنْكَ وَمَا تَنْظُرُ، وَتَحْسَبُ أَنَّكَ جِرْمٌ صَغِيرٌ، وَفِيكَ انْطَوَى الْعَالَمُ الْأَكْبَرُ، وَأَنْتَ الْكِتَابُ الْمُبِينُ الَّذِي بِأَحْرُفِهِ يَظْهَرُ الْمُضْمَرُ</span> W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t a beautiful — your cure is within your own self. Your cure is within yourself, but you don''t realize it. Your sorrows are from your own actions, but you don''t see it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَ تَحْسَبُ أَنَّكَ</span>‏ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جِرْمٌ</span>‏ <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَغِيرٌ وَ فِيكَ انْطَوَى الْعَالَمُ الْأَكْبَرُ</span> You think you''re insignificant? You <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve an entire universe operating within you. Look at the structure of a human being. Look at the complexity of a human being. Look at the DNA. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنْتَ الْكِتَابُ الْمُبِينُ الَّذِي بِأَحْرُفِهِ يَظْهَرُ الْمُضْمَرُ</span> You are the clear book of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, every word of which is revealing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span> secret.</p>
<p>Do you think a human being <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s been created in vain, just came out from evolution, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he doesn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve import, he''s just ashes to ashes, dust to dust? You think the human being doesn''t <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve importance? So in the eyes of the Prophet, a human being was important, and a human being in submission is even more precious. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, he said, killing a believer unjustly is more atrocious with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n destroying the entire <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْكَعْبَة</span> brick by brick. Human beings are important, they''re not insignificant, and this is w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was letting people know, like his concern for people. And he honored everyone, irrespective of their color, creed, race. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t didn''t matter to him. His company was constant education. They used to say, . If we came upon him suddenly, we would go in a state of awe. . But when we were sitting with him in his company, spending time with him, we fell in love with him. And this is not just for the Muslims, right? Because we know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t the emperor of Kisra, of Persia, used to send messengers to the Prophet.</p>
<p>And these messengers, they said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t when they came in the presence of the Prophet, they felt more awe t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n they did in the palaces of Kisra. So when you look at the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of this man, you <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve to fall in love with him. A scholar said t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t if the Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d no other miracle t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n the men and women he produced, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t would be enough as his miracle. If he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d no other miracle, not the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, nothing else, but just the men and women t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he produced through his <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, through his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is enough as a miracle. He took simple, primitive people. He turned them into the greatest thinkers, people willing to die for him, the greatest people of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَدَبٌ</span>, of erudition. You know, people of culture. They weren''t scholars. They weren''t aristocrats, you know, with these great backgrounds and formal education. No. Simple, simple people.</p>
<p>Hazrat Bilal Habshi, he becomes a giant among men. And he was an Abyssinian slave. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ</span>. You know, my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>la, just a few days ago, Anisa <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>la, she s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>red the story of Hazrat Bilal with me, and I looked it up, and incredible story. So the story goes t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t they asked Bilal, &quot;How did you meet the Prophet for the first time?&quot; So he says, &quot;One day I was very sick and I was lying in bed and my master saw it as laziness and it made him angry. So he dragged me...&quot; And Hazrat Bilal <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>d a really cruel master. And so he said it made him angry. So he dragged me out in the cold of the night — while he''s sick, right? Keep in mind he''s sick. He <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s a high fever.</p>
<p>He drags him out in the cold of the night and makes him take his shirt off as a punishment for not being at work. So Bilal says t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, you know, he told me to get to work, to grind the sack of wheat. So he said, &quot;I started crying.&quot; You know, and this man walked up to me and asked me, &quot;Why are you crying?&quot; He didn''t know t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this was the Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. He just thought it was another man. He''s a slave. Bilal said, &quot;I''m sick, I can barely walk, and my master <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>s given me these sacks of wheat to grind, and if I don''t do it by morning, he''s going to beat me.&quot; So the Prophet got up and he left, and Bilal says, &quot;I thought in my heart, all these people, they come and they want to know about my pain, but nobody is willing to help.&quot; And he says, &quot;As I was thinking this, this man returns to me with warm milk in one <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>nd and some medicine in another.&quot; And Bilal says he gave it to me and he told me to go to bed. And he said, &quot;I can''t do t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t.</p>
<p>You know, if I''m not done grinding this wheat into flour, I''m gonna get beaten in the morning.&quot; Prophet said, &quot;Don''t worry about it.&quot; So Bilal said, &quot;I saw the Prophet grind the wheat all night while I slept and rested.&quot; And then he again returned the next night with milk and medicine and did the same thing again. He spent all night grinding the wheat for Bilal while he slept and rested. Bilal says he did this for three consecutive nights until I regained my health. An Abyssinian slave. Why does he <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve — w<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t benefit is he getting out of doing t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t? But this was our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, and the love it generated in Bilal''s heart after t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t.</p>
<p>So just look how he took these less t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>n ordinary people and he turned them into these giants. Abu Zar G<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ffari, he was a Bedouin, he belonged to Bani G<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ffar. These guys were brigands, they used to rob highways. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s the tribe he belonged to. And this was the tribe t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t wrote the book on vengeance. You know, nobody messed with the Bani G<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ffar. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t is the tribe he was from. They were ruthless people. And look at Abu Zar G<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ffari.</p>
<p>He becomes one of the greatest companions and scholars of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span>, of Maulana Ali. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. Look at Abdullah ibn Masud. Look at this man. He was a shepherd in the hills of Mecca. A shepherd. Go look at a shepherd. Have you ever met a shepherd? Seriously. Simple people. Not scholars. No big ideas, really.</p>
<p>They''re very simple people. They don''t think about a whole lot. Ibn Masud, a shepherd, he becomes a teacher of an entire generation. He teaches an entire — 75% of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيثٌ</span> and the meanings of those are from Abdullah ibn Masud. An entire generation of Muslims come from the school, the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span> of Ibn Masud, who came from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَدْرَسَة</span> of the Prophet. Just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, just c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, just the company of the Prophet.</p>
<p>I mean, I can go on and on about the stories just telling you about the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of a prophet, but you know, I''m gonna end with this one last thing about him t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t I want to s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>re with you t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t sums up his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, for me at least, of how he was prophesized in earlier books. The advent of the Prophet, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t he was going to come, was mentioned in previous, all previous religious books. But so I want to s<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>re t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t, how he was described, but I''m not going to translate it for you for the want of time. But you know, there is this — in these previous books, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> describes all of his qualities by which he will be recognized, or by which we can, you know, he was telling the Christians, the Jews, how you can recognize him. And he said, so it goes like something like...</p>
<p>So those of you who knows Arabic, they can probably follow t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t. You know, I''m gonna raise a man from amongst you. ... <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْحِكْمَةَ مَنْطِقُهُ، وَالسِّتْرُ وَالْوَفَاءُ طَبِيعَتُهُ، وَالْعَفْوُ وَالْمَعْرُوفُ خُلُقُهُ، وَالْحَقُّ شَرِيعَتُهُ، وَالْعَدْلُ سِيرَتُهُ، وَالْهُدَى إِمَامُهُ، وَالْإِسْلَامُ مِلَّتُهُ، وَأَحْمَدُ اسْمُهُ، هَدَى بِهِ بَعْدَ الضَّلَالَةِ، وَعَلَّمَ بِهِ بَعْدَ الْجَهَالَةِ، وَرَفَعَ بِهِ بَعْدَ الْخَمَالَةِ، وَعَرَّفَ بِهِ بَعْدَ النَّكِرَةِ، وَأَكْثَرَ بِهِ بَعْدَ الْقِلَّةِ، وَأَغْنَى بِهِ بَعْدَ الْعَيْلَةِ، وَجَمَعَ بِهِ بَعْدَ الْفُرْقَةِ، وَأَلَّفَ بِهِ بَيْنَ أُمَمٍ مُتَفَرِّقَةٍ، وَقُلُوبٍ مُخْتَلِفَةٍ، وَأَهْوَاءٍ مُتَشَتِّتَةٍ</span> Beautiful, beautiful wordings. I mean, we could just, it''s gonna take so much time to even explain this, but just describing these incredible qualities of the Prophet. But after he revealed all of these beautiful c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racteristics,</p>
<p>and the qualities of the messenger, who''s going to be sent for humanity so t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t nations could identify him. After mentioning all this, in the end he says, he identifies the most prominent c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of this man. It says, you will see t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t this man will walk with such humility, the greatest man in the world, the beloved of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>, the leader of all messengers, t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t you will see him walk with such humility t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t if he walked on dry leaves, you would not hear them crack under his feet. To me, this sums up the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of my Prophet. This humility, the sense of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span> before <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. Seeing people as the creation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span>. May <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ</span> strengthen us all, our children, our generation, you know, with the c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter of this incredible Prophet. He is such a school. We <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>ve so much to learn from him. You know, today is his birthday. Today is the day we were blessed with his presence.</p>
<p>This day is not so t<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t we just talk about all of this and forget about it the next day. This 12th <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِيعُ الأَوَّل</span> is not to be celebrated on one day. It is meant to be celebrated every single day of our lives. And the way we celebrate our messenger is by exemplifying his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter, by cultivating his c<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>racter within our own self. By constantly thinking, how would my Prophet interact here? By knowing about him, by learning about him, by studying his interactions. How did he deal with this situation? W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t did he do? W<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t did he say in this situation? T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s celebrating the Prophet. This day, this 12th <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ الْأَبَلْ</span> is supposed to be celebrated every single day of our life.</p>
<p>It''s not just, you know, we say all this good stuff about him and then just go on with our lives as it was. T<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُبُّ النَّبِيِّ</span>t''s not celebrating. We can celebrate the rest of the world like this, not our Prophet. We should celebrate him every single day of our lives by remembering him, by knowing about him, by learning about him. ...........................</p>
