UPDATE chapter SET html = html || '<p class="ar-block"><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ، بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ، الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ خَالِقِ الْوُجُودِ مِنَ الْعَدَمِ، وَجَاعِلِ النُّورِ مِنَ الظُّلَمِ، وَمُخْرِجِ الصَّبْرِ مِنَ الْأَلَمِ، وَمُلْكِ التَّوْبَةِ عَلَى النَّدَمِ، فَنَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى الْمَصَائِبِ كَمَا نَشْكُرُهُ عَلَى النِّعَمِ، وَنُصَلِّي عَلَى رَسُولِهِ الْأَكْرَمِ، لِلشَّرَفِ الْأَشَمِّ، وَالنُّورِ الْأَتَمِّ، وَالْكِتَابِ الْمُحْكَمِ، وَكَمَالِ النَّابِغِينَ وَالْخَاتَمِ، سَيِّدِ وَلَدِ آدَمَ، الَّذِي بَشَّرَ بِهِ قِسُّ بْنُ مَرْيَمَ، وَدَعَا لَهُ بِأَسَاطِيحِهِ إِبْرَاهِيمُ عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ، حِينَ كَانَ يَرْفَعُ قَوَاعِدَ بَيْتِ اللَّهِ الْمُحَرَّمِ، فَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَعَلَى آلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ، وَعَلَى أَصْحَابِهِ خَيْرِ الْأُمَمِ، الَّذِينَ بَارَكَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ كَافَّةَ النَّاسِ الْعَرَبِ مِنْهُمْ وَالْعَجَمِ، وَصَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى أَمِيرِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ الْأَنْزَعِ الْبَطِينِ وَقَائِدِ الْغُرِّ الْمُحَجَّلِينَ، غَالِبٍ عَلَى كُلِّ غَالِبٍ، وَمَطْلُوبِ كُلِّ طَالِبٍ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي أَنْزَلَ عَلَى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتَابَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَهُ عِوَجًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًا وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ شَرِيكٌ فِي الْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُنْ لَهُ وَلِيٌّ مِنَ الذُّلِّ وَكَبِّرْهُ تَكْبِيرًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ الَّذِي نَحْمَدُهُ وَنَسْتَعِينُهُ وَنَسْتَغْفِرُهُ وَنُؤْمِنُ بِهِ وَنَتَوَكَّلُ عَلَيْهِ، وَنَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنْ شُرُورِ أَنْفُسِنَا وَمِنْ سَيِّئَاتِ أَعْمَالِنَا، مَنْ يَهْدِهِ اللَّهُ فَلَا مُضِلَّ لَهُ، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلْهُ فَلَا هَادِيَ لَهُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَنَشْهَدُ أَنْ لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، وَنَشْهَدُ أَنَّ مُحَمَّدًا عَبْدُ اللَّهِ وَرَسُولُهُ، أَرْسَلَهُ اللَّهُ تَعَالَى بِالْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ الْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُ عَلَى الدِّينِ كُلِّهِ وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ شَهِيدًا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ لَا عِلْمَ لَنَا إِلَّا مَا عَلَّمْتَنَا إِنَّكَ أَنْتَ الْعَلِيمُ الْحَكِيمُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ عَلِّمْنَا بِمَا يَنْفَعُنَا وَانْفَعْنَا بِمَا عَلَّمْتَنَا</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّا نَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ، وَمِنْ قَلْبٍ لَا يَخْشَعُ، وَمِنْ عَيْنٍ لَا تَدْمَعُ، وَمِنْ نَفْسٍ لَا تَشْبَعُ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَأَمَّا بَعْدُ، قَالَ اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَىٰ فِي كِتَابِهِ الْكَرِيمِ، بَعْدَ أَنْ قَالَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ مِنَ الشَّيْطَانِ الرَّجِيمِ بِسْمِ اللَّهِ الرَّحْمَٰنِ الرَّحِيمِ</span>: <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي، وَيَسِّرْ لِي أَمْرِي، وَاحْلُلْ عُقْدَةً مِنْ لِسَانِي، يَفْقَهُوا قَوْلِي</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ بِـلَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْنَا مِنَ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا بِالصَّبْرِ، آمِينَ يَا رَبَّ الْعَالَمِينَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَةُ اللَّهِ وَبَرَكَاتُهُ</span></p>
<p>So as I mentioned today, I just wanted to end these sessions, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ</span>, for the opportunity to be able to talk about our Prophet. And, you know, thank you all for joining and taking the time out. I know we have such busy schedules, but may <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> reward you for making this a priority. Today''s session, like I said, is more geared towards my kids in the session. I have my nephews, my nieces, and then I''m sure there are many other young adults in the group that I don''t know of. But I hope that this session will help you in some way and make things easy for you.</p>
<p>So I wanted to end this with a model of success. The dictionary defines success as the accomplishment of an aim or a purpose. So if we are all going on a bus and I invite you young boys and girls in this group to join me on that drive. And let''s say we drove by this beautiful mansion, right?</p>
<p>A stunningly beautiful house on top of a hill with this magnificent sports car outside and an incredible backyard with an Olympic size swimming pool in it. And if I asked you guys, do you think the guy living in this mansion is successful? Overwhelmingly, I think your answer is going to be the yeah, right? So let''s say we continue driving and a little further we see a man, a homeless man living in a cardboard box on the street. And the clothes that he''s wearing appears to be like a couple years old. Now, if I asked you, is this man successful? What would you say? I''m pretty sure your answer will be no.</p>
<p>And that''s the problem. The problem is in the way we think about success and the way in which we think about failure. Now, when we turn to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, we find that one of the most beautiful, one of the most captivating, magnificent palaces that were ever built was the castle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْنُ</span>.</p>
<p>It was one of the most amazing palaces ever built. Was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فِرْعَوْنُ</span> successful in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>? No, he wasn''t. According to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, he was amongst the worst of losers, the royal losers. What was the second kind of person we met on our trip? That homeless man, right? Was <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْرَاهِيم</span> kicked out of his house? Yes. Did <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُوسَى</span> have to flee from his city and live a poor life in a foreign land? Sure. But were they both failures? No. Not according to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. According to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, they were both extremely successful people. So when I see this world through the lens of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, my perspective changes. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is teaching me that a homeless man is successful. And an incredibly wealthy man, an incredibly powerful man is a complete failure in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>.</p>
<p>This means success in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> has nothing to do with fame. It has nothing to do with power, wealth. It has nothing to do with how beautiful you look or how many likes you get on Facebook. In the same way, failure from <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s perspective has nothing to do with poverty or destitution. So then what is success? There are several words for success that are used in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. The most commonly used are <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَوْز</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاحٌ</span>. Those are the two words that you must have come across quite often if you recite the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. The word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَوْز</span>, it means victory. Like when you win a competition or generally when there is some kind of prize involved, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَوْز</span> is used. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تِلْكَ حُدُودُ ٱللَّهِ ۚ وَمَن يُطِعِ ٱللَّهَ وَرَسُولَهُۥ يُدْخِلْهُ جَنَّتٍۢ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَرُ خَلِدِينَ فِيهَا ۚ وَذَلِكَ ٱلْفَوْزُ ٱلْعَظِيمُ</span> ‎. Whoever obeys <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His Messenger is going to enter paradise that has rivers flowing under it. They will live there forever.</p>
<p>That is a great victory. So it creates this image, the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَوْز</span>, it creates this image like a prize, right? Paradise is being given as a prize at the end of the journey. But <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاح</span> is a much more powerful word in Arabic. It is actually an agricultural term. The word means to till the ground, to plow the land, to cultivate it. What do you do when you till the land? Like a farmer, when he tills the land, what is he trying to do? He unfolds and he reveals its intrinsic properties of growth. When you till the land, you break open the surface of the earth and you activate its power of productivity. That''s imamate of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاح</span>.</p>
<p>When a farmer works for months on his farm tilling the land, cultivating it, laying the seed, watering it, protecting it from insects over and over again, month after month, what happens eventually? He gets a harvest, which is many times more, exponentially more than his original investment. That is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاح</span>. That''s the concept of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَاح</span>. That is success. To receive a return on your investment that is beyond measure.</p>
<p>Now one of the motifs of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> is the use of oaths. Right? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> swears by things in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. We see this in many verses in which he does that. And the purpose behind these, and this is a very complex subject, I''m just going to sort of browse over it, but it''s very interesting why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> takes oaths. But to summarize, the purpose is that to grab our attention. That''s the first purpose. And second, to emphasize the point that he''s about to make. And always, you know, oath is taken of sanctified things, right? We take oaths of sanctified things, things that are important. If I said, you know, I swear to you by this mosquito in my room, or I swear to you by the stain on my carpet, or this ripped t-shirt, you know, it''s not going to generate serious confidence in you, is it? About what I''m about to say. But if I swear by things that are valuable to me, right? I swear by my daughter, I swear by the life of my son, then it increases confidence and it adds value to the reason and purpose of my taking that oath. Now, what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one taking the oath, right?</p>
<p>We understand its importance in the context of human beings, but now what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is the one taking the oath? How would that grab your attention? And how much trust and importance would you place on what he''s about to say? And what if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> swears not by one object, but multiple objects, multiple sanctified objects? How would that affect you? You see my point? So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> takes some oaths in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الشَّمْسِ</span> over some objects that are really important for human life. Keep in mind the importance of an oath as you hear this. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلشَّمْسِ وَضُحَىٰهَا</span>, I swear by the sun and its brilliance. First oath, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلْقَمَرِ إِذَا تَلَىٰهَا</span>, and by the moon as it follows. Second oath, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلنَّهَارِ إِذَا جَلَّىٰهَا</span>, and by the day as it brightens things.</p>
<p>Third oath, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاللَّيْلِ إِذَا يَغْشَاهَا</span>, and by the night as it conceals things. Fourth, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَٱلسَّمَآءِ وَمَا بَنَىٰهَا</span>, and the heavens and the one who built it. Fifth, and by the earth and the one who spread it. Sixth, and by the soul and the one who perfected it. Seventh, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> takes seven oaths using 14 objects, right? The sun and its brilliance, the moon and its following the sun, the day and its luminance, the night and its concealment of things. The heavens and the one who built it, the earth and the one who spread it, the soul and the one who perfected it. Seven oaths using 14 objects of oath before coming to his point. So whatever he''s about to say, how important and valuable do you think it is going to be? And what is it that he wants us to know? He has revealed what is wrong and what is right.</p>
<p>So he makes this declarative statement, and then he comes to the point. See that word? Indeed, the one who purified himself succeeded. That same word is used, exponential returns on investment. And the one who corrupted himself has failed. So successful is he who purified his soul during his lifetime, and the one who corrupted his soul is the ultimate failure.</p>
<p>So with that definition of success, I wanted to share with you these 10 qualities that were identified by a neuropsychologist who studied successful people over a period of several years. And he found certain qualities that were common in them. Now the principles of success, right? If they''re good for this world, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>, then they should also be good for the spiritual success to me, because that''s what principle means to me, universal truths. So I''ve been trying to follow some of these and I''ve seen some really tangible improvements in my life. So I thought I''d share these with you, hoping and praying that they''ll help you as well.</p>
<p>Again, this is just <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَصِيحَة</span>, it is just a suggestion, take it or leave it. But at least give it a shot is what I would say because, you know, I''ve learned not to reject ideas without trying them first. Anyway, so these qualities that he mentions, the first quality of success he says is that successful people have a strong sense of purpose. In order to succeed, you have to have a strong sense of purpose. What is our purpose as Muslims? Our purpose is our Lord. He is our ultimate purpose. It is the recognition and our willing submission to our Creator. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَا خَلَقْتُ ٱلْجِنَّ وَٱلْإِنسَ إِلَّا لِيَعْبُدُونِ</span> ‎. We have not created jinn and man except for recognizing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> as the only one worthy of our devotion and worship. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَآ إِلَهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّهُ</span>. That''s our purpose. And what greater sense of purpose than to know that our goal is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>?</p>
<p>What greater purpose than to emulate the beautiful character of our Prophet? And the essence of his character was humility. That is the key to success in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. Humility in our dealings with the Creator and humility in our dealings with his creation.</p>
<p>The second most identifiable character, he said, is that successful people seek out role models or mentors. And these mentors instill in them a sense of possibility. This is the reason why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> was given the stories of previous prophets, you know, those prophets that came before him, to give him <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَثْبِيت</span>, to make his heart firm. So that he would know that he would have success just as the prophets before him had success despite all their tribulations. So role model, having a right role model is extremely important for success. Look who we follow today, right? Look at our role models today. Hollywood and Bollywood stars, singers, actors.</p>
<p>Our culture is filled with these icons. These false idols that are set up for people to imitate. Not great people, not people of high <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, not people of great character. Actors, musicians, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُشَاوِزٍ</span>, people of the lowest level of human society in history. If you study history, Roman history, Greek history, any history in the old time, you''ll see that the lowest people in any society have been actors and musicians, the jesters in the courts. There''s nothing noble about these professions. And yet, in today''s society, they are exalted, they are worshipped, and they''re put up on this pedestal for us to follow. You have the best example to follow in the character of your Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. What impresses you about these people? Seriously, what impresses you about these actors that makes you wanna imitate them, be like them? What is it?</p>
<p>Because they display these beautiful human qualities on a screen, on a movie, of love, of courage, of charity, of generosity, of patience, resilience in the face of adversity. Is that what attracts you about them? They were acting, just acting. They could be wretched in their real lives, and many of them are. My prophet was not an actor. My prophet was not pretending to have these beautiful qualities that capture the heart and mind. He was living them daily. He was the embodiment of every truth that has ever come to mankind, and in following him we are elevated.</p>
<p>You know, today the world is going insane over wanting to be like someone, right? They want to be like, because to them being is being like, when you don''t have an essence of your own. You know, like all these people out there imitating pop stars because they don''t have a personality of their own. They''re empty people. So they take the shells of other people, because being for them is being like.</p>
<p>They have no reality of their own. So Michael Jackson wears one glove, they start wearing one glove. If Stallone starts wearing Ray-Ban glasses, they start wearing Ray-Ban glasses. And this is a disease. You know, we as Muslims, read the history. We as Muslims used to be trendsetters. People used to imitate us. But look how we''ve fallen. So you want a role model to follow? I''ll give you a role model. His name is Muhammad Mustafa <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَآلِهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. Follow him.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلِيُّ بْنُ أَبِي طَالِبٍ</span>, another role model. Not an actor, not a pretender, not just acting to be courageous. So really take some time to think about who you want to follow and why. I say this with real seriousness. I mean, you guys are our future. You are the future of our religion. And we have high hopes from you. Seriously, we have high hopes from you. I have high hopes from you. So be the light in these dark times for those around you. Be a role model for your friends by adopting the character of your Prophet, and you will find success in this life and the next. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>The third quality they say is the strength of visualizing goals. No one had more clarity of his goal than our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. You know, visualizing ourselves in the presence of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Visualizing ourselves in the presence of the Prophet, of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَولَانَا عَلِي</span>, of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَئِمَّة</span>. You know, visualizing that they are greeting us with happiness and pride and not with disappointment. You guys have seen that Lord of the Rings, right? There''s that scene in that movie where that king falls in battle, I forget his name, and he''s about to die. And his daughter is crying next to him. I remember he says to his daughter, don''t be sad. In that scene, he says to his daughter as he''s dying, don''t be sad. I go to my fathers, in whose mighty company I shall now not feel ashamed. What a powerful statement that is, right? I go to my fathers, in whose company I shall not feel ashamed.</p>
<p><span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>. When I die, when I leave this world, that''s what I want to be able to say to my daughter. That''s my vision. That I''m going in the company of my prophet. I''m going in the company of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَولَانَا عَلِي</span>, the Imams, and I''m not ashamed. I don''t feel ashamed. That is what we want, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>. So our vision is the vision of success. We are people of success. And this religion is a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> of success. It is not a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينٌ</span> of failure. And our prophet was the most successful human being that ever lived on the face of this earth.</p>
<p>The fourth, he said, is a positive sensitive orientation. That we dwell on past successes and not on past failures. That we never give up hope. That we never stop striving no matter what challenges confront us. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> reminds us. Remember your past successes, when you were weak and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> gave you victory in the battle of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَدْر</span>. And so we have to remember that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> will give us victory in the end if we keep trying and if we don''t give up. You know, I''ll tell you something about setting goals. You know how everybody tells you, when you hear this, dream big, reach for the sun, the moon, the stars. Don''t. If you want my sincere advice, don''t reach for the sun. Think small.</p>
<p>Don''t think big. Reach for the light bulb on your ceiling. That''s an awesome goal. Because what happens is you get so excited and you take on this enormous burden, this entire weight of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>ic practices in one day, and then you collapse under its weight. Like going into a gym and trying to bench press 300 pounds on your first day. That''s crazy. So my advice to you, find small, manageable goals. That''s what works for me. If you''re not praying at all today, start with one single prayer. If you''re praying once a day, try adding another one onto it. Take on one quality of the Prophet you want to adopt and work on it for a while before you pick up another. And then celebrate your success, seriously.</p>
<p>Enjoy your success. Feel great about doing that one extra prayer today instead of beating yourself up about not doing three. Seriously, I promise it''ll work wonders for you. And I might get into a lot of trouble for saying this, but the things I do for you guys. You know, once the Prophet was at a place and he wanted to set up a fire, he asked his companions to spread out and bring water. No, actually, he told them to spread out and bring twigs and branches that they could find. I think it''s in <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدَّارِمِي</span> as well. So they all went out and they picked up these small little things and they piled it on the ground. And soon a huge pile was formed. And he turned to his companions and he said, look, this is how good deeds accumulate. And this is also how bad deeds accumulate. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>.</p>
<p>So work on collecting these tiny twigs, tiny branches of good deeds, of good character. One more prayer, one more fast, understanding one more verse of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, one more act of courage, one more act of generosity, of temperance, one more small act of patience, not losing it, one more realization of gratitude, right? And before you know it, you''ll have this huge pile of good deeds in your collection, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>. So that''s another goal, that positive sensory orientation and a focus on it is important. The fifth he says is self-assurance. To know and believe that you can succeed. Do not feel weak, do not grieve, for you are great if you are believers, if you are believers. So have the confidence and assurance that you will succeed in the end. We have an everlasting life of joy and happiness ahead of us, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِن كُنتُم مُّؤْمِنِينَ</span>, if you are believers. So study your religion, learn about it, because knowledge builds certainty.</p>
<p>Knowledge builds confidence. It builds self-assurance. Get your knowledge from your own studies, not from what the media tells you. Not from what your friends are tweeting about. Take it from the source, from the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. The next he says is that successful people, they plan and organize. So start your day with a spiritual plan. What are you going to do today? How will you manifest the truth today? Or how will you conceal it? Are you going to follow <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ</span> today? Or will you follow <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِبْلِيس</span>?</p>
<p>What will you prioritize today? Is it going to be your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> or is it going to be fun and entertainment? And once you''ve established that, work everything around it. No, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رَسُولُ اللهِ صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he did not spend his day in seclusion on the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُصَلًّى</span> with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَسْبِيح</span> in his hand all day long. No, he was out there enjoying the gift of life. But he knew what his priorities were. So successful people know how to prioritize. They break down their goals into workable, achievable parts. So we have to look at the most important things in our lives and we have to take it, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخْلَاق</span>, our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِبَادَة</span>. We have to learn to prioritize our day around these things and not the other way. That if I have time, I will do <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>.</p>
<p>No, I will do my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span> and if I have time, I will do something else. Our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دِينُ الإِسْلَامِ</span>, our debt of submission has to come first and foremost. So get the essentials in. Seriously, I know people arguing over ingredients in their toothpaste and not concerned about their daily prayers. They get caught up in this, these trivial matters. There''s this show these days going on, right? A friend of mine, right? He watched, we were watching this show and I got up for prayers and I went and I came back. He was still watching the show and he says, wow, you know, my faith is renewed. And all this. And I said, what kind of, you know, fresh <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is this that you see me getting up for prayers and it doesn''t move you to pray? But yeah, just taking all that nice feeling about, oh, how beautiful <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is, but then not getting up to pray.</p>
<p>What kind of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is that? Because the last I checked, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِيمَان</span> is work. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is hard work. And they get caught up in these, you know, even like in these discussions, you get caught up in these trivial matters. Get the essentials in before you worry about the trivial things. Because this was the disease of that Talmudic rabbinical mind, which asked what kind of cow? What color of cow? Fat or thin? Right? In <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ</span>? And they say that the devil is in the details, right? Even in today''s culture, we say that.</p>
<p>These are narrow-minded, simpleton people that spend their lives in trivial pursuits, in matters of no concern. We have greater things to prioritize, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>, like fasting, like <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زَكَاة</span>. Our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> was a universal man. He raised us above these trivialities and he put us into the most important matters. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمَ لَا يَنْفَعُ مَالٌ وَلَا بَنُونَ إِلَّا مَنْ أَتَى اللَّهَ بِقَلْبٍ سَلِيمٍ</span>. The day that nothing will benefit them, not wealth or anything except the one who comes with a sound heart, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قَلْبٍ سَلِيمٍ</span>. And so worry about where your heart is in your prayer, not where the feet is, not what color topi you''re wearing. Where''s the heart? Is it with God or is it with Amazon going over your shopping cart? Or is it with Netflix wondering what''s going to happen in the next episode?</p>
<p>You know, the other day somebody was telling me that people get upset with this person if she wishes them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جُمُعَة مُبَارَك</span>. Amazing, right? They don''t get upset when they lie, when they cheat, when they gossip, betray people''s trust. No. They get upset when someone wishes them <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَوْمُ الْجُمُعَةِ</span>. Oh, you know, this is a bit... this is an innovation. I mean, seriously, you''re going to get offended and upset for somebody wishing you the blessings of Friday. And that''s the big priority instead of worrying about the prayer of Friday. Seriously, we''ve just gone mad. You know, is it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ</span> or is it <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السَّلَامُ عَلَيْكُمْ</span>? Oh my God.</p>
<p>Seriously, you know, should I put a space after the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحُبّ</span> before I put the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَلَامٌ</span> in? I''ll tell you what you can put there. It''s just, oh my God, I can''t tell you how. So, we have to learn to prioritize. The only reason, you know, these big corporations are so successful is because they know how to plan and how to organize. They plan way ahead of time. We can''t even plan for tomorrow, right? The only thing some of us have become good at is just planning what we''re going to eat for the next meal.</p>
<p>The next one he says is the ability to acquire the necessary skills needed to succeed. And that means you have to identify where your weaknesses are. And one of our greatest weakness is that we read the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, but we don''t understand it. I think that to me personally, I think that''s one of the biggest problem.</p>
<p>How is it that as Muslims, we have not examined the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? People taking pride over, you know, my son, my daughter is six months old and she completed like five <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Like that''s the biggest thing today with the recitation of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. What about the understanding of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? What about understanding the meaning behind <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? You know, so many of us have this romantic attachment to the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Oh, what a great book. But we''ve never understood it. We read it like a parrot, but we never take the time to understand it. What''s the point? This is a book of guidance. How do you get guidance from a book you don''t even understand?</p>
<p>Right? We''re like people in this amazing restaurant looking at this menu written in Chinese. How are we going to order? When are we gonna be bothered to taste the beauty of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>? And this is why this romantic attachment to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is such a flimsy attachment. It''s an attachment that leads to extremism. It''s an attachment that leads to blind passion, right? And passion, once you get passionate about something, then everybody knows passion is something that is incompatible with the intellect, right? Like that story of that sparrow on top of Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَان</span>''s place of worship. So for those of you who don''t know that, there was this sparrow. He was trying to convince a female sparrow that he was a worthy mate. And she wasn''t paying any attention to him.</p>
<p>And this is a story, right? He said, &quot;You know, if I wanted, I''d turn this dome right over on the head of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَان</span> for you.&quot; And <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُلَيْمَان عَلَيْهِ السَّلَامُ</span> knew the interpretation of birds, so he called him down. And so he comes down, you know, all shaking, his little sparrow heart beating in his little sparrow chest, and Sulaiman says, &quot;What made you say that ridiculous remark?&quot; And the sparrow says, &quot;O Prophet of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, don''t you know that lovers are never taken to account for what they say in the presence of their beloved?&quot; In other words, I was in a state of passion. You can''t take me to account for that. And this is the reality around us, right? We get so these passionate displays of things and we have absolutely no idea what we''re doing, what we''re reading. It''s just amazing.</p>
<p>So build your skillset, study the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, study the meanings of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُعَاء</span>, study the meanings of these supplications you say in your prayers, right? There are resources available. I mean, I did it. I learned the whole <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> without, like most of it, I started off with my own self just by learning, you know, Google. And then I went to teachers and I had, you know, found the proper resources and did that. So can you. But do it slowly. Don''t have to do the whole thing in one day, gradually. And don''t worry how long it takes. Who cares? You have your whole lives ahead of you, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَا شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ</span>.</p>
<p>So all I''m saying is get started. Start collecting these small twigs, but do it consistently and before you know it, you will be reciting with comprehension and gaining something from it. The next quality is that they are patient people, he said. They have . Be patient and enjoining others to patience. I''ll tell you, there are two lessons as far as I''m concerned that we have to learn. Because all of the tribulations coming to us are based on two things as far as I''m concerned. One is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, arrogance and pride, and the other is lack of patience. Arrogance and the lack of patience. Until we get rid of this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كِبْرٌ</span>, this sense of superiority from our hearts and recognize that it is humility, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَادَّ</span>, that elevates us in the eyes of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>. Not arrogance and pride.</p>
<p>Unless we do that, we are never going to be successful as individuals. We''re never going to be successful as a community. And we have to learn to be patient. This disease of just no patience at all, instant gratification, it''s destroying our spirituality. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is nothing if not delayed gratification. Our gratification is in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخَرَ</span>, not in this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">دُنْيَا</span>. And we have to learn to persevere. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَن لَّيْسَ لِلْإِنسَنِ إِلَّا مَا سَعَىٰ</span> ‎. No one has except what they endeavor for, what they persevere for. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَنَّ سَعْيَهُۥ سَوْفَ يُرَىٰ</span> ‎. Indeed, you will see the efforts of your endeavor. On the Day of Judgment, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّ هَذَا كَانَ لَكُمْ جَزَآءًۭ وَكَانَ سَعْيُكُم مَّشْكُورًا</span> ‎. This is the reward for you and your efforts have been shown gratitude by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. We want <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s appreciation.</p>
<p>We don''t want this world''s praise. What good is that? How long is it going to last? And if we follow the teachings of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span> and if we follow the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُنَّة</span> of our prophet, we can have both. Look at our prophet. 1400 years later, people are willing to die for his name. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سُبْحَانَ اللَّهِ</span>, that''s <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سَيْزَا</span>. That''s glory, that''s fame, that''s respect. ‏<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَمَنْ أَرَادَ ٱلْآخِرَةَ وَسَعَىٰ لَهَا سَعْيَهَا وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌۭ فَأُو۟لَٓئِكَ كَانَ سَعْيُهُم مَّشْكُورًۭا</span> ‎, whoever desires the hereafter strives for it, right? Putting in the required work, he is a believer. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَانَ سَعْيُهُم مَّشْكُورًۭا</span>, his efforts will be appreciated, they will be thanked and rewarded by God. So that''s the other one, you know, work on removing arrogance from your hearts and developing patience cause you guys, you don''t even let anybody say anything to you before you just explode.</p>
<p>So just take a chill pill, right? Relax. Then the last thing he said is the ability to love what one is doing. You have to love what you''re doing. They love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> loves them. They''re pleased with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is pleased with them. And none of us truly believe until we love <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and his messenger more than we love our own selves. And so working for <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> and his messenger <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> should be the most beloved thing to us. So this is the last quality, you have to love what you are doing. This is not a forced religion. Ours is not a forced religion. There is no compulsion in religion.</p>
<p>There is no force in religion. Ours is a religion of love. It is a religion of willing submission, not forced submission. And this is why I said earlier that this religion begins with a choice. I mean, you have to decide if <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is for you or not. And if it is, then you have to make a conscious choice to accept it and then enjoy it. You have to love your <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span>. If you''re doing it, if you''re following <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> with a resentment and a frustration or out of fear of getting burned in hellfire, there''s something very wrong with your understanding of this beautiful religion, I swear. I can honestly tell you, I love my <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> and I''m not a hermit. I''m as much out there living and enjoying life as the next person. I love hiking.</p>
<p>I keep going on trips all year long. But I try to prioritize my essentials. Every day I try to work on my character, adopt something good, leave something bad. And I fail more often than I succeed. Honestly, I fail more often than I succeed. So what? I try to celebrate what I did succeed in. And I try harder to work on what I failed at the next day. And thank God, I have friends who keep reminding me when I struggle. Those are the kinds of people you want in your life. People who lift you up when you''re down. Not those who want you to stay small.</p>
<p>There are many people who want you to stay small. There''s Marianne Williamson, right? I''ve talked about her before also. Love her. In her book, she has this quote which is just incredible. There''s a book, Return to Love, that she wrote, and she says in that, our deepest fear is not that we are inadequate. Our deepest fear is that we are powerful beyond measure. It is our light, not our darkness, that frightens us most. We ask ourselves, who am I to be brilliant? Who am I to be gorgeous, talented or fabulous? Actually, who are you not to be? You are a child of God.</p>
<p>Your playing small does not serve the world. There''s nothing enlightened about shrinking so that other people won''t feel insecure around you. We were born to make manifest the glory of God that is within us. It''s not just in some of us. It''s in everyone. And as we let our own light shine, we unconsciously give other people the permission to do the same. As we are liberated from our own fear, our presence automatically liberates others. What a beautiful description of what a, like, I love this quote from this lady. So yeah, that is our, you know, don''t stay small, you are. You know, you are manifesting the glory of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>, this <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خَلِيفَة</span> on this earth. Don''t sell yourself short. So these are the qualities that he mentioned about success.</p>
<p>And you know, it all begins with waking up from our state of acedia, the spiritual laziness that I talked about earlier, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> is work. Anything that is valuable, beneficial, worth pursuing in your life. Tell me one thing that does not require work. Why would <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> be any different? But we are in the state of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَفْلَة</span>, this heedlessness, like drowned in it. There''s a verse in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, we have prepared for the hellfire multitudes from the jinn and human beings. And he says, what has made you know, and I''m saying this, that what has made these people eligible for hellfire, he describes that. He says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لَهُمْ قُلُوبٌۭ لَّا يَفْقَهُونَ بِهَا</span>, they have hearts and they have intellects but they do not use them. They don''t use their intellects to reflect on what''s really good for them. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُمْ أَعْيُنٌۭ لَّا يُبْصِرُونَ بِهَا</span>, they have eyes but they don''t see the true realities with it. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلَهُمْ آذَانٌ</span>, they have ears, but they don''t listen to the words of guidance. Then he says, these people are like dumb beasts. They are like cattle and sheep. But then <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says, he says no. And this no here is rhetorical. In Arabic, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلْ</span> is used to clarify something.</p>
<p>So if you made a mistake or said something that was not clear enough, you use the word <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَلْ</span> to re-emphasize. So <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> is saying these heedless people are like animals. But let me clarify further. They are much worse than animals. Why? Because they are in a state of heedlessness. Now if you look at the animal kingdom, you will not find animals in a state of heedlessness. Animals, no matter what they are doing, they are always in a state of awareness. If you watch a dog eat or a rabbit eat, right? Rabbits, right? They''re constantly moving their ears around.</p>
<p>Why? Because they''re worried. They''re constantly worried about these external threats, external dangers. Watch a mouse come out of its hole. It doesn''t just saunter out of its hole. It comes out slow. It moves its head around, right? Checks out the surroundings. To me, that is <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>. Seriously, you wanna learn <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span>? Watch a mouse come out of its hole. That''s God consciousness.</p>
<p>That''s how we need to be, that level of awareness of God. We can learn a lot from animals, right? That''s why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> uses them as metaphors in the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>. Look at the lizard. Ever seen a lizard come out of its hole? It''s constantly looking back and forth, right? Right and left. The Arabs say more bewildered than a lizard. Because if you look at a lizard, it''s just constantly moving its head because it, like, it''s bewildered. But what is it really doing? It''s checking. Is there danger out there?</p>
<p>It''s thinking. Animals are constantly aware of danger. Why? Because if they''re not constantly aware, their existence is threatened. They realize that if they are in a state of heedlessness, they are endangering themselves. And this is why <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> says these people are like animals. In fact, they are more astray than animals because animals are not in a state of heedlessness about the danger that is threatening them. But we human beings, constant state of heedlessness about our spiritual survival. We don''t care. Seriously. Animals, if they sense fire, they run. What do we do?</p>
<p>We call each other up. Hey, there''s this huge fire there. Let''s go check it out. Right? Taking selfies in front of the fire. I''m not making this up, right? This happens or not. Because it''s all fun. It''s all entertaining. Everything. We''re drowned in fun and entertainment. Everything is a joke.</p>
<p>Everything is a fun. The world is fun. The <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَخَّرَ</span> is fun. And we''re not realizing that we are dying spiritually. We are dying. People, your oppression is only harming you. It''s killing you. We have to change. I mean, how much selfishness can go on? I want you kids to grow up in a world that facilitates your humanity. I want a world for you that doesn''t destroy your humanity or take it away from you. I want a world that does not deprive you of this quality, right? It''s your birthright. It''s your birthright to become a human being, to not operate at the level of animals. It''s your birthright to become real people. Not to become junk people that spend their lives in these trivialized malls buying all this junk crap to fill their junk life. When are we going to wake up? When are we going to respond to the call of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His messenger? <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا اسْتَجِيبُوا لِلَّهِ وَلِلرَّسُولِ إِذَا دَعَاكُمْ لِمَا يُحْيِيكُمْ</span>. O you who believe, answer <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His messenger when they call you to the path that will give you life. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِذَا دَعَاكُمْ لِمَا يُحْيِيكُمْ</span>, when they call you to that which gives you life, that will make you feel alive. When are we going to answer that call? When are we going to respond to that call of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His messenger to something that will bring us to life? And the implication in that <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيَةٌ</span> is that as long as we do not respond to that call, we''re dead people. Literally, we are dead people. Our hearts are dead. Our lives are dead.</p>
<p>Our society is a society of death. We live in a society that celebrates the death of the spirit in giving life, in giving life to the lowest pursuits of humanity. This is the death of the soul because the soul can only be brought back to life by recognizing what it was created for. For recognizing what its purpose is and then setting out and striving to achieve that goal. Recognizing that we will have shortcomings. That we will make mistakes. That those mistakes are part of our humanity. That <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّهُ سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span> knows those mistakes. So he will forgive us when we turn to him. That is why we were created. We were created to respond to our <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَبُو ضِيَاء</span>, our servitude to <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه سُبْحَانَهُ وَتَعَالَى</span>. And the only way, the only way a slave feels its <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدِّيَ</span>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'a model for success';
UPDATE chapter SET html = html || ', its servanthood, is in its abject nature, that if we do wrong we desire the atonement so that we can reconnect with our true nature. This is why we were given the gift of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وُدُّهُ</span>. This is why we were given the gift of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَاةٌ</span>. What beautiful gifts.</p>
<p>What a beautiful gift to purify ourselves five times a day and stand before our Lord desiring atonement for our sins and for strength for our future actions. What a gift. We are just wasting away the preciousness of our lives. I mean, you have one short life to do something, to do something great, to be great people, one short span. Look what people are doing to relieve the stress around them, to just to relieve this, this empty meaningless life, just to taste this one moment, to have an adrenaline rush, to feel alive. That''s all they''re trying to do. They''re trying to feel alive because they''re dead. They are dead people. And by jumping off a bridge with a rubber string attached to them and seeing this pavement, this cement pavement, come towards them, heading right for it, suddenly this person feels for one moment that he''s actually alive, and then he wants to repeat that experience because he wants to feel alive. That is not the life human beings were created for. It''s not through adrenaline rushes. It''s not through the rush of drugs penetrating your brain. It''s not through the rush of cocaine.</p>
<p>It''s not through alcohol numbing your mind. It''s not through the rush of jumping out of an airplane or skydiving. Those are not the things that will make you feel alive. The only thing that will make you feel alive is becoming a slave of the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الحُبُّ</span>, by becoming slaves of the ever living who never dies. And this is our challenge. This is the challenge of human beings and this is what we have to wake up to. I''m going to end this today''s session with the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> from our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>. And you know, and this is, this whole conversation, everything I''ve said so far is for you guys, you kids, you young adults, which I keep saying because, you know, the elders already know all of this. But this I thought was something which might really help you. I hope and pray that it helps you.</p>
<p>There''s a <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَدِيث</span> of our Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span>, he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> began as a stranger and there shall come a time when it becomes estranged again, just the way it began. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> began as a stranger and towards the end of the time it will become a stranger again. Strange here does not mean weird. Strange does not mean something out of this world. It means that the society we live in, the society that you live in, is going to find <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> and its followers strange. Why? Because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> began at a time when people were chasing and worshipping their desires, their appetites. They were worshipping multiple gods. Might was right. The rich were honored and the poor were abased. Evil was strong and good was weak. That''s how <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> began.</p>
<p>Today, we see that we''ve come full circle. The world is once again worshipping their desires. The world is once again in submission to the gods of money, god of fame, the god of power, the god of stature, the god of looks, of pride, vanity. Might is once again right, evil is once again ruling the world, and righteousness is once again weak and on its knees. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بَدَأَ الإِسْلَامُ غَرِيبًا وَسَيَعُودُ غَرِيبًا كَمَا بَدَأَ</span>. <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِسْلَام</span> began as a stranger, and towards the end of time, it will become a stranger again. But then he said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُوبَى لِلْغُرَبَاءِ</span>. There is immense good tidings for the estranged. When you are strange, you act in ways that are very different from what everybody else around you is doing. That''s what makes you strange. When you wake up in the night to worship <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, when the world around you is sound asleep, you are strange.</p>
<p>When you speak about pleasing <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and making him your objective in life, when the world is making fun of religion and mocking God, you are strange. When <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His Prophet is your sole objective, because <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> said, O Messenger of <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>, tell them that indeed <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> should be their ultimate objective. So when you have made <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> as your objective in a world that has the fulfillment of their desire as their primary objective, you are strange. When you dress modestly in a world that celebrates nudity, you are strange. When you refuse to smoke, to drink, to do drugs, when the rest of the world is celebrating and glorifying these things, you are strange. When you help a needy with your charity in a world that is hoarding wealth, you are strange. When you are saving yourself from marriage, when the entire world is celebrating fornication, you are strange. When you respect your parents, when you respect your elders, your teachers, when the entire world is finding it so cool to disrespect them, to make fun of them, you are strange. When you honor <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span>''s gifts of food and drink, when you conserve natural resources while the world is busy wasting and destroying them, you are strange. When you stay humble in a world that celebrates arrogance, that celebrates pride, vanity, you are strange.</p>
<p>When you take out time to recite and study and understand the <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُرْآن</span>, and you make time to learn about your prophet while the rest of this world is drowned in chasing the world or endless hours of mindless Netflix and Prime Video, you are strange. And my Prophet <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ</span> said, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">طُوبَى لِلْغُرَبَاءِ</span>. There is glad tidings, good tidings for the estranged. So my advice to you is better be a stranger in this temporary life than a stranger with <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His Messenger on the Day of Judgment. We do not want to be people on that day that says, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا وَيْلَنَا مَنْ بَعَثَنَا مِنْ مَرْقَدِنَا</span>. Woe be unto us. Shame be unto us. Why have we been raised from our graves? I wish we were never raised from our graves. We don''t want to be that people. We don''t want to be the people who say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا وَيْلَنَا هَذَا يَوْمُ الدِّينِ</span>. Woe to us! This is the day of judgment that we had been warned about and we never paid attention to it. We don''t want to be that people.</p>
<p>We don''t want to say to our Lord, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَيَقُولَ رَبِّ لَوْلَآ أَخَّرْتَنِىٓ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍۢ قَرِيبٍۢ</span>. Master, if you send us back in the world, we promise we will believe in everything that you said, and we will become righteous. No, we want to be people who are greeted and welcomed by <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اللَّه</span> and His Prophet in the afterlife. We want to be people that they say, <span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَيَّتُهَا النَّفْسُ الْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ ارْجِعِي إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ رَاضِيَةً مَرْضِيَّةً</span>. Oh my beautiful and content soul, return to your master in a state that you are pleased with your Lord and he is pleased with you. Enter the company of my beloved slaves, enter my paradise. (<span class="ar-inline" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ، وَالصَّلَاةُ وَالسَّلَامُ عَلَى رَسُولِهِ سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ الْخَاتِمِ النَّبِيِّينَ سَيِّدِ الْمُرْسَلِينَ، مُصْطَفَاهُ وَوَاسِعِ الْبِشَارَاتِ الْوَاسِعِينَ</span>)</p>' WHERE slug = 'love-of-the-prophet' AND anchor_key = 'a model for success';
